  ],
  "activity_sections": [
    {
      "key": "3c91611a-1685-46cb-a12e-47da899a31d2",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Pre-Assessment"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3c91611a-1685-46cb-a12e-47da899a31d2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9d40b440-f413-4e83-8ee3-19bb01ce8f1e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c7213e07-a7cb-4050-ae2b-6a392013d8c4",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c7213e07-a7cb-4050-ae2b-6a392013d8c4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9d40b440-f413-4e83-8ee3-19bb01ce8f1e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "98e10d1b-c8b3-43b6-b1cd-903c065aefaf",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students recognize that keeping track of changing information in order can be difficult without a clear system.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students read the starting list of tickets. Then click through the animated slide to display the \"Then\" steps in order at a steady pace without pausing long enough for students to fully solve each step. After all steps have been revealed, ask students to determine the final order of the tickets. Have them turn and talk with a partner to compare answers and explain how they kept track, then invite a few students to share their reasoning.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may lose track of the tickets or give different answers. Don't correct immediately! Instead, ask how they kept track and what made it difficult to keep track of the changes."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "98e10d1b-c8b3-43b6-b1cd-903c065aefaf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b1cbcc3d-2b38-41db-a466-386263de65ee"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2f8a5dae-7c47-4db6-a2ab-fb59fcaa782d",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2f8a5dae-7c47-4db6-a2ab-fb59fcaa782d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b1cbcc3d-2b38-41db-a466-386263de65ee"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c00367ed-23fa-4c1f-b736-6afb18841230",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c00367ed-23fa-4c1f-b736-6afb18841230",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b1cbcc3d-2b38-41db-a466-386263de65ee"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7e52870e-8713-4875-8a8a-c1205e719213",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Model how a system stores and updates an ordered collection of data using positions and values.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Put students into groups of four.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each group a copy of the Concert Entry Line activity guide, a copy of Problem cards (Problems 1-5), and sticky notes.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the activity scenario and clearly establish the model students will use. Explain that they are managing a line that must stay in order while people are added, removed, or moved. Be explicit that each chair represents a position in the line and that the person sitting in that chair is the value being tracked. Emphasize that their job is to keep the line accurate as changes happen.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Concert Entry Line",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students will often confuse the person with the position, especially early in the activity. Reinforce multiple times that the chair is the position and the person is the value. When circulating, ask questions like \"Which chair is that?\" and \"Who is sitting in that position?\" to help them maintain the distinction."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7e52870e-8713-4875-8a8a-c1205e719213",
        "lesson_activity.key": "22118563-1031-4c13-beb0-ce7bfd1694cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "257905ca-b01b-4a6d-b05b-32477011a78c",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "257905ca-b01b-4a6d-b05b-32477011a78c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "22118563-1031-4c13-beb0-ce7bfd1694cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b19ee1eb-ffc0-4121-a518-ed7acb381912",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Guide students through setting up their concert line. Have each group choose a few members to represent the line, then arrange themselves and sit in the numbered chairs starting at position 0. Emphasize that each chair represents a position and that every position should be clearly labeled. Let students know it is okay if some chairs at the end are empty.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students who are not labeling positions, are counting people instead of using chair numbers, or are confusing the person with the position. If groups are stuck or inconsistent, prompt them with questions like \"Which position is this?\" or \"If this person leaves, does the chair disappear—or does someone else sit in it?\" Focus on helping them establish a stable structure before they begin the problems.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may focus on arranging people rather than tracking positions. Reinforce that the goal is to keep track of positions accurately, not just organize the line. When students get stuck, avoid telling them what to do—ask questions that push them to reason about what changed and what stayed the same."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b19ee1eb-ffc0-4121-a518-ed7acb381912",
        "lesson_activity.key": "22118563-1031-4c13-beb0-ce7bfd1694cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d47906d0-2668-4e67-be69-abbfacba7688",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d47906d0-2668-4e67-be69-abbfacba7688",
        "lesson_activity.key": "22118563-1031-4c13-beb0-ce7bfd1694cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "411c711f-d8b5-4c86-9ed9-cb2395d7b111",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to begin working through the problem cards using their activity guide. For each problem, they should read the card, discuss their approach as a group, update their concert line, and record their changes. Leave this slide visible while students work so they can refer back to the process.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Watch for students who are not using the chair numbers to track positions, are counting people instead of using structure, or are confusing the position with the person. If groups are stuck, ask questions like \"Did the position change or just the value?\", \"Who moved?\", or \"What stayed the same?\" to push their reasoning.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may focus on physically moving people rather than tracking information accurately. Reinforce that their goal is to keep the system correct, not just rearrange the line. Avoid telling students exactly what to do—use questions to guide them toward the correct reasoning. If time is tight, you can stop after Problem 4 and move to the debrief."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "411c711f-d8b5-4c86-9ed9-cb2395d7b111",
        "lesson_activity.key": "22118563-1031-4c13-beb0-ce7bfd1694cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b1da6c53-c7e5-4e40-9264-f7a706d69f51",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b1da6c53-c7e5-4e40-9264-f7a706d69f51",
        "lesson_activity.key": "22118563-1031-4c13-beb0-ce7bfd1694cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "19c35cee-6c09-407e-9490-858555daa102",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *When did only one position change?*\n- *When did many positions change?*\n- *How did you keep track of who was in each position?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students compare different types of changes and recognize that sometimes only one position changes, while other times many positions shift. They understand that positions stay fixed when a value is replaced, but shift when the structure changes, and that using positions helps them track the system accurately.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may focus on the physical movement rather than the underlying structure. Push them to describe what happened to the positions, not just who moved. If students give vague answers, ask follow-ups like \"Did the position change or just the person?\" or \"Who else had to move because of that?\" to sharpen their thinking."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "19c35cee-6c09-407e-9490-858555daa102",
        "lesson_activity.key": "22118563-1031-4c13-beb0-ce7bfd1694cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ed1c0e09-8882-46a0-a923-049097693f4a",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ed1c0e09-8882-46a0-a923-049097693f4a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "22118563-1031-4c13-beb0-ce7bfd1694cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e611c16d-bc22-485c-9130-f0396ae1e039",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Briefly summarize what students did during the activity.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Reinforce that the chair represents the position and the person represents the value. Students will mix this up if it is not consistently reinforced."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e611c16d-bc22-485c-9130-f0396ae1e039",
        "lesson_activity.key": "22118563-1031-4c13-beb0-ce7bfd1694cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e2190709-9aa0-4fa2-acd7-8fce801f9036",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e2190709-9aa0-4fa2-acd7-8fce801f9036",
        "lesson_activity.key": "22118563-1031-4c13-beb0-ce7bfd1694cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "595ce41b-d328-4b2e-b836-f14afe5be082",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *array*. Emphasize that what students just worked with—a line of people tracked by position—is the same structure as an array. Make the connection to variables from the previous unit and that unlike a variable which can only store one item at a time, arrays can store multiple items, so we give each item an index to help keep track. Keep the explanation brief and focused on confirming their understanding rather than introducing new ideas.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, ask: \"Where do you see the chairs and people in this diagram?\" to reinforce the connection to the activity. Students may focus on the examples (numbers, words, images) and miss the underlying structure, so continue to emphasize that arrays track values in order using positions (indices)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "595ce41b-d328-4b2e-b836-f14afe5be082",
        "lesson_activity.key": "22118563-1031-4c13-beb0-ce7bfd1694cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b1861da2-dc2a-4c8a-8c2b-1046eca82c82",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b1861da2-dc2a-4c8a-8c2b-1046eca82c82",
        "lesson_activity.key": "22118563-1031-4c13-beb0-ce7bfd1694cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dc525e23-71cb-4a42-a44e-4dea4057a188",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Briefly connect arrays to AI by explaining that clear, well-organized data helps both programmers and AI tools understand what belongs together. Emphasize that when arrays are messy or out of order, AI may give incorrect or confusing suggestions because it relies on patterns in the data."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dc525e23-71cb-4a42-a44e-4dea4057a188",
        "lesson_activity.key": "22118563-1031-4c13-beb0-ce7bfd1694cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4e02f49c-fb42-4fbe-afcf-0d3cc7959dcf",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Image you asked AI to help debug your concert line. What would be easier for AI to understand: a clearly ordered array or a messy list where the positions keep changing without explanation?",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4e02f49c-fb42-4fbe-afcf-0d3cc7959dcf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "22118563-1031-4c13-beb0-ce7bfd1694cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cfbb8513-6c8e-438d-a8ee-a546a5d6a934",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Connect the activity to programming concepts. Walk through the first few mappings with students and connect them directly to what they experienced—for example, the line represents the array, the chair positions represent indices, and the person in each chair represents the item. Then have students work with a partner to interpret the remaining mappings before briefly confirming as a class.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may try to match words without understanding the meaning. Push them to explain each mapping in their own words by asking questions like, \"What did the chair represent?\" or \"What does the number of people tell us in code?\" Focus on helping them connect the physical model to the abstract concept, not just memorize the terms."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cfbb8513-6c8e-438d-a8ee-a546a5d6a934",
        "lesson_activity.key": "22118563-1031-4c13-beb0-ce7bfd1694cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ed545568-e22e-4733-a0c8-5a4de06981c7",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ed545568-e22e-4733-a0c8-5a4de06981c7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "22118563-1031-4c13-beb0-ce7bfd1694cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bd41923a-fe1b-4af7-817e-f8c696fa0c75",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the code by pointing out that this line creates an array and gives it a name. Focus on the variable name concertLineArray and connect it back to the activity by explaining that this represents the concert line they were just working with.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Point to one name in the list and explain that each name inside the brackets is an item in the array. Then point out that each item has a position, called an index, which determines where it is in the list. Connect this back to the activity by reminding students that this is like each person sitting in a specific chair in the concert line.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that we can use .length to find out how many items are in an array."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bd41923a-fe1b-4af7-817e-f8c696fa0c75",
        "lesson_activity.key": "22118563-1031-4c13-beb0-ce7bfd1694cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1aa984f3-ebbd-45c5-9441-a7a2210b6050",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Practice representing and updating ordered data in code using arrays, indices, and .length.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 through 5.\n- Level 1: Students declare an array.\n- Level 2: Students access values using indices.\n- Level 3: Students update array values.\n- Level 4: Students use .length to find the size of the array.\n- Level 5: Students complete a Check for Understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for whether students are connecting the code to the model from the activity. Ask questions like:\n- \"If you asked AI for help on these levels, what exact part of your array would you need to show or describe so it could help you accurately?\"\n- \"Which part of your code is the list?\"\n- \"Where are the positions (indices)?\"\n- \"What does this value represent?\"",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Working with Arrays",
        "progression_name": "Working with Arrays",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students struggle with .length - 1, quickly sketch a small array with indices and show how the last position relates to the length before sending them back to code."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Levels 1 through 5 can be used as a formative assessment of the learning objectives:\n- Represent an ordered collection of data using an array\n- Access and update values in an array using indices\n- Use .length to determine the size of an array"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1aa984f3-ebbd-45c5-9441-a7a2210b6050",
        "lesson_activity.key": "22118563-1031-4c13-beb0-ce7bfd1694cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "82964130-9cd6-4997-979e-8156c865742a",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "82964130-9cd6-4997-979e-8156c865742a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "22118563-1031-4c13-beb0-ce7bfd1694cc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f7ada4d9-3659-450e-8345-4f69405ebfba",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students turn and talk with a partner to share their ideas. Encourage them to explain how arrays help track and update information, using examples from the concert line activity or the code they just worked with. After partner discussion, invite a few students to share out with the class.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f7ada4d9-3659-450e-8345-4f69405ebfba",
        "lesson_activity.key": "774105cf-153d-4952-a34d-b01a79115123"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "da195c93-f57b-4805-a947-d3d1686c28b5",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Help students notice that familiar apps use a consistent structure behind the scenes to display data.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *Can you tell which data might be personal or private?*\n- *What data might you not want to share with AI?*\n- *If you gave this data to an AI system, what might it do with it?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that this is the user profile of a music streaming app that shows information about a particular user including their Top Tracks. Emphasize that some fields (names, membership type, location) are more sensitive than others, and that apps should separate or hide these fields to protect users. Emphasize that personally identifiable information (PII) like emails, phone numbers, or exact locations should not be shared with AI at all. Push students to think not just about what data is sensitive, but how AI systems might use or misuse that data (e.g., storing, sharing, or making inferences).\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Briefly synthesize the warm-up discussion by introducing these guidelines for sharing data with AI. Emphasize that AI tools can store, reuse, or make inferences from data, so developers must be intentional about what they share. Walk through each bullet and connect it back to the user profile example, reinforcing that personally identifiable information (PII) should never be shared and that example or fake data should be used instead when testing.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Optional extension: Have students identify one example from the profile on the previous slide and classify it as  “safe to share” or “not safe to share,” explaining their reasoning."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "da195c93-f57b-4805-a947-d3d1686c28b5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0ced5957-3ce2-4a07-b100-c3c1ed854eac"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "da785c6c-c6b0-4ff6-af76-a76b8f232669",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "da785c6c-c6b0-4ff6-af76-a76b8f232669",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0ced5957-3ce2-4a07-b100-c3c1ed854eac"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "10a771b8-a14f-469e-9dac-ac2a7afbc609",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "10a771b8-a14f-469e-9dac-ac2a7afbc609",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0ced5957-3ce2-4a07-b100-c3c1ed854eac"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cf7e6e78-80c7-4627-9df9-5896b2896126",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Help students organize the information they identified into a clear plan by defining the fields and data types needed for each item.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 2, Level 1. Explain that they are not coding yet; their job is to observe how a card, list, or profile displays information and identify what pieces of data each item needs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students explore, ask: “What information appears for one item?” “Which pieces of data repeat across items?” “Which value controls the image?” and “Which value controls the title or text?” Encourage students to look for patterns that will help explain how the app keeps related data connected.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Planning and Structuring Data",
        "progression_name": "Explore a User Interface (UI)"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cf7e6e78-80c7-4627-9df9-5896b2896126",
        "lesson_activity.key": "803d1cba-0df1-4d4c-9e59-e76d699c1619"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "62882ca6-391d-407d-a1bf-2fdb3e2277b9",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What information is needed for one item?*\n- *What pieces of data repeat across items?*\n- *How do you think the app keeps those pieces connected?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to identify that each item in the UI is made up of multiple related pieces of data (e.g., title, image, text) that repeat across items. Help them recognize that these pieces must stay connected and organized in a consistent structure, which sets up the need for arrays and indexed data in the next part of the lesson.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Use this slide to transition from the UI exploration to planning. Remind students that each item they explored (card, list, or profile) required multiple pieces of information to display. Introduce the contact card as a new example and model how to identify the fields (what information is needed) and data types for one item before students begin planning their own data.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, ask students to compare the UI they explored with the contact card. Ask “What pieces of data did each item need? How is this contact card similar?”"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "62882ca6-391d-407d-a1bf-2fdb3e2277b9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "803d1cba-0df1-4d4c-9e59-e76d699c1619"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7fc5b4ac-b55b-4474-b61c-e7a7453d172c",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7fc5b4ac-b55b-4474-b61c-e7a7453d172c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "803d1cba-0df1-4d4c-9e59-e76d699c1619"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fc5e90bd-60e1-47b9-ad54-29afa4bb5fa7",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define data type by connecting it to the fields students just identified. Explain that each piece of information (like a name, number, or image) has a specific type, and use the examples on the slide to show how different kinds of values are categorized. Keep this brief and focused before moving back into how these types are used in their data plan.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, ask students to identify what type the “name” field on the contact card would be? What about a profile picture?"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fc5e90bd-60e1-47b9-ad54-29afa4bb5fa7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "803d1cba-0df1-4d4c-9e59-e76d699c1619"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b1ea71af-d9aa-4a36-8380-ba424e7f22fe",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b1ea71af-d9aa-4a36-8380-ba424e7f22fe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "803d1cba-0df1-4d4c-9e59-e76d699c1619"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "561934c2-5d4d-43e3-b5b4-8a870bd2571e",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce .map() briefly as a way to apply the same action to each item in a list. Connect it to the UI examples students explored by explaining that apps use patterns like this to build each item (card, list, or profile) from structured data. Keep this explanation short and let the following slides show how it works in context.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animation and connect this code to the UI examples students explored. Explain that each array stores one type of information (names, times, images), and that the same index is used across all arrays to keep those pieces connected for each item. Emphasize that .map() runs through one array and uses the index to pull matching data from the others.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Focus student attention on the two parameters inside the parentheses—item and index. Explain that item represents the current value being processed, while index tells the code which position it is on. Emphasize that index is what allows the program to connect matching data across multiple arrays. Ask students, “If we’re at index 1, what three pieces of data are being connected?”\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Use the table to show how the index connects related data across arrays. Walk through one row at a time (starting with index 0) and point out how the values from each array combine to represent one complete item. Emphasize that the index is what keeps all the pieces aligned.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Before revealing the full example, ask students to predict which values will be grouped together at a specific index (e.g., “What will index 1 look like?”)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "561934c2-5d4d-43e3-b5b4-8a870bd2571e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "803d1cba-0df1-4d4c-9e59-e76d699c1619"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "34e9c1a5-4bdb-4d4d-a266-00bfaca75e23",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "34e9c1a5-4bdb-4d4d-a266-00bfaca75e23",
        "lesson_activity.key": "803d1cba-0df1-4d4c-9e59-e76d699c1619"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0c699acb-79b9-41bf-bc10-52bf3b1a6f3b",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Connect the createCard() function to the visual example by walking through how one item is built. Click through the animation and show how the values at a single index are combined to create one complete card. Emphasize that .map() repeats this process for every index to generate all items in the display.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Briefly explain the role of helpers.js by connecting it to what students saw during exploration. Point out that functions like createCard() are stored separately to keep the main code simpler. Emphasize that helper functions handle repeated tasks so the program can stay organized and easier to read.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Before students generate arrays with AI, use this slide to review what they should check. Walk through each question and connect it to their plan: the data structure should match what they designed, arrays should stay aligned in length and order, and the output should match what they expect to display. Emphasize that students should check AI-generated code before accepting and using it, rather than assuming it is correct.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, ask students which of these checks would catch a mistake where data doesn’t line up correctly."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0c699acb-79b9-41bf-bc10-52bf3b1a6f3b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "803d1cba-0df1-4d4c-9e59-e76d699c1619"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fbe4ce86-7e86-4a4c-9676-31a50b79b9c0",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fbe4ce86-7e86-4a4c-9676-31a50b79b9c0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "803d1cba-0df1-4d4c-9e59-e76d699c1619"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "14713e72-96ec-4e80-87b0-ae90c2071f41",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Use arrays and AI-generated data to build and display items, ensuring data is structured correctly and aligned across arrays.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 2, Level 2 to complete Levels 2 through 6.\n- Level 2: Students choose a UI component (list, card, profile) to map data in Sketch Lab.\n- Level 3: Students utilize AI to generate arrays of data that match their UI.\n- Level 4: Students use arrays to display one item by selecting values at the same index.\n- Level 5: Students use .map() to display all items instead of just one.\n- Level 6: Students answer a Check For Understanding question.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students are not using real personal data and are labeling fields with clear data types. Ask, “Which of these fields would you actually show on-screen?” Look for evidence that students working are through these levels step by step. As students debug their code, make sure they align array lengths and replace hard-coded indices with loops. Ask, \"How can you make sure each index lines up across arrays?\" Check that students are evaluating AI code suggestions rather than just copying them. Ask, “Why did you accept or reject that AI suggestion?”",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Building and Displaying Data",
        "progression_name": "Turning Data into Displays"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "14713e72-96ec-4e80-87b0-ae90c2071f41",
        "lesson_activity.key": "803d1cba-0df1-4d4c-9e59-e76d699c1619"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5401b185-f14d-4c1d-9b6c-52ae3ef80250",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students who finish early to help peers by asking them clarifying questions about their data fields. For example, “Does every item have this field?”"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use student answers on Level 6 to check who understands how parallel arrays work and how AI might make a mistake when generating or displaying data."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5401b185-f14d-4c1d-9b6c-52ae3ef80250",
        "lesson_activity.key": "803d1cba-0df1-4d4c-9e59-e76d699c1619"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "990692ff-57d6-4b5b-9549-569f4293ba71",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "990692ff-57d6-4b5b-9549-569f4293ba71",
        "lesson_activity.key": "668efd88-b5f0-485e-b967-fa3bff08af9f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3a9ed7b3-129f-457e-958c-f064e0bd73d5",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Activate students' prior intuition about adding/removing items so they can connect it to array methods.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Reintroduce the Concert Line from Lesson 1. Remind them that this represents an array. Have them share predictions for what they think each of the methods might do.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3a9ed7b3-129f-457e-958c-f064e0bd73d5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1d5e1c4b-d5fb-49df-8183-46e9fcb77e80"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c66ec1a9-683e-428c-a5fc-a4697ed21263",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 3, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 through 2. Remind students to click each button multiple times, watch how size and order change, and use AI to explain what each function does. Emphasize that they should compare the AI’s explanation to what they actually observe in the app and code.\n- Level 1: Explore code for a party playlist manager, use AI to explain each function, and evaluate whether the AI explanation matches what they observed.\n- Level 2: Reflect on how the methods change arrays and how related arrays must stay in sync.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** On Level 1, check that students are testing each button, using AI to explain the code, and comparing the AI’s explanation to what they observe. Ask: “What changed in the array?” or “Did the AI explanation match what you saw?” On Level 2, check that students can describe how the methods affect size and order and recognize that related arrays must be updated together.\n",
        "progression_name": "Explore Array Methods"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c66ec1a9-683e-428c-a5fc-a4697ed21263",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0503bf65-6b4e-4850-9e7f-cae72c7e252a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "713597b5-b852-4f61-b452-06ea6ac3e52b",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Reflection: Array Methods",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use student explanations on Level 2 to check whether they recognize that all four methods add or remove items at the ends of arrays and must be applied consistently across parallel arrays.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "713597b5-b852-4f61-b452-06ea6ac3e52b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0503bf65-6b4e-4850-9e7f-cae72c7e252a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "21b0d2e0-3c80-433e-ad20-d3e19f2eac91",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "21b0d2e0-3c80-433e-ad20-d3e19f2eac91",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0503bf65-6b4e-4850-9e7f-cae72c7e252a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "032fdd56-32ce-4e70-b9ff-ec322ea30919",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Sometimes AI gives answers that sound right, but aren’t actually correct. That’s because it’s predicting patterns, not checking the code.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "032fdd56-32ce-4e70-b9ff-ec322ea30919",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0503bf65-6b4e-4850-9e7f-cae72c7e252a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ea1959ec-5dc5-48f6-bae9-47cc9e4952b2",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that the AI tutor may give explanations that sound correct but are not accurate. Have students compare what the AI said with what they actually observed in the app and code. Then ask: “So what actually changed in the array?” and follow up with: “What pattern do you think the AI was following that made it say that?” to push students to explain the reasoning behind the mistake.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may trust the AI explanation without checking it. Push them to use the code and behavior as evidence, and to explain why the AI might have made that mistake. Reinforce that AI is a tool to help them think—not something to blindly trust.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ea1959ec-5dc5-48f6-bae9-47cc9e4952b2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0503bf65-6b4e-4850-9e7f-cae72c7e252a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c32b7459-d22b-4088-b60d-3a3b19295c3e",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c32b7459-d22b-4088-b60d-3a3b19295c3e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0503bf65-6b4e-4850-9e7f-cae72c7e252a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "069275ef-727e-4f61-ba95-ddef052c56d5",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students a simple routine for evaluating AI explanations. Walk through each question and model how to compare what the AI said with what actually happened in the program. Emphasize that students should use the code and program behavior as evidence to confirm or challenge the AI’s explanation before moving on.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "069275ef-727e-4f61-ba95-ddef052c56d5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0503bf65-6b4e-4850-9e7f-cae72c7e252a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "beb2d693-3b3b-4806-a457-a0f1e5351f9f",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What actually changed in the array with each method - the size, order or both?*\n- *Did the change happen at the beginning or the end of the array?*\n- *Did the AI explanation match this?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to describe what changed in the array after each method and to identify patterns in how size, order, and position are affected. Help them connect these observations to where the change occurs (beginning or end of the array), and evaluate whether the AI explanation accurately described these changes. Emphasize that students should use evidence from the code and program behavior—not just the AI explanation—to support their reasoning.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "beb2d693-3b3b-4806-a457-a0f1e5351f9f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0503bf65-6b4e-4850-9e7f-cae72c7e252a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b8394e5c-9a95-4fd4-8597-197af6663bdc",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk students through the push() method using the example on the slide. Before revealing the result, give students a moment to predict what the new array will be and record their answer in the Unit Guide. Then reveal the updated array and discuss how push() adds a new value to the end and increases the size. Connect this to the concert line from lesson 1 as a person joining at the end, then have students complete their Unit Guide notes.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b8394e5c-9a95-4fd4-8597-197af6663bdc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0503bf65-6b4e-4850-9e7f-cae72c7e252a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "42b41b92-5c82-4cd8-b15c-d92cdc6db5b9",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk students through the pop() method using the example on the slide. Before revealing the result, give students a moment to predict what the new array will be and record their answer in the Unit Guide. Then reveal the updated array and discuss how pop() removes the last value and decreases the size. Connect this to the concert line as the last person leaving, then have students complete their Unit Guide notes."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "42b41b92-5c82-4cd8-b15c-d92cdc6db5b9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0503bf65-6b4e-4850-9e7f-cae72c7e252a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "45561ab9-3836-4d1d-a7b7-6f24f31076a4",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk students through the shift() method using the example on the slide. Before revealing the result, give students a moment to predict what the new array will be and record their answer in the Unit Guide. Then reveal the updated array and discuss how shift() removes the first value and causes all remaining values to shift one position to the left. Connect this to the concert line as the first person leaving and everyone moving forward, then have students complete their Unit Guide notes."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "45561ab9-3836-4d1d-a7b7-6f24f31076a4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0503bf65-6b4e-4850-9e7f-cae72c7e252a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bccb5028-245a-43e8-8243-e6d8415d93c1",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk students through the unshift() method using the example on the slide. Before revealing the result, give students a moment to predict what the new array will be and record their answer in the Unit Guide. Then reveal the updated array and discuss how unshift() adds a new value to the beginning and causes all existing values to shift one position to the right. Connect this to the concert line as a new person joining at the front and everyone moving back, then have students complete their Unit Guide notes."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bccb5028-245a-43e8-8243-e6d8415d93c1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0503bf65-6b4e-4850-9e7f-cae72c7e252a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "61e4a97f-18e7-4188-a88a-b9f265e1e102",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that many apps use multiple arrays to store related information, where each index represents the same item across arrays. Walk through the example and show what happens if only one array is updated. Emphasize that the same operation must be applied to each array to keep the data consistent.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may think arrays operate independently. Reinforce that in real applications, arrays often work together, and incorrect updates can break the relationship between data.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "61e4a97f-18e7-4188-a88a-b9f265e1e102",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0503bf65-6b4e-4850-9e7f-cae72c7e252a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3a535168-9c88-4964-9e63-6881647b403e",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3a535168-9c88-4964-9e63-6881647b403e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0503bf65-6b4e-4850-9e7f-cae72c7e252a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7f401602-ed7a-4d09-9188-aff2068ec86d",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 3, Level 3 to complete Levels 3 through 8.\n- Level 3: Students write a function using push() to add a task to the end\n- Level 4: Students write a function using unshift() to add a task to the beginning\n- Level 5: Students write a function using shift() to remove the first task\n- Level 6: Students write a function using pop() to remove the last task\n- Level 7: Students identify and fix bugs where arrays become out of sync\n- Level 8: Students reflect on the bug by comparing their hypothesis, the AI’s suggestion, and explaining why the mistake happened\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** In Levels 3–6, check that students are using the correct array method, updating all arrays consistently, and testing their code. Ask: “Which method are you using”, “What do you expect to happen?”, or “What actually changed?” In Level 7, look for where data becomes out of sync. Ask: “When does it break?” or “What caused it?” In Level 8, check that students compare their thinking with the AI and explain the mistake. Ask: “Why did the bug happen?” and “How did you fix it?”",
        "progression_name": "Build a To Do List App",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Look for students who can:\n- correctly use push, unshift, shift, and pop\n- keep parallel arrays in sync\n- identify and fix desynchronization bugs\n- explain why the bug happened, not just how they fixed it\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Look for students who can:\n- correctly use push, unshift, shift, and pop\n- keep parallel arrays in sync\n- identify and fix desynchronization bugs\n- explain why the bug happened, not just how they fixed it\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7f401602-ed7a-4d09-9188-aff2068ec86d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0503bf65-6b4e-4850-9e7f-cae72c7e252a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "622497b4-ac28-4dc5-aa93-cb9cea961d30",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "622497b4-ac28-4dc5-aa93-cb9cea961d30",
        "lesson_activity.key": "0503bf65-6b4e-4850-9e7f-cae72c7e252a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "57a0c108-c341-48dc-ac2a-a29421e4e429",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students turn and talk with a partner to share their ideas. Encourage them to explain how array methods help manage collections by describing what changes in the array when items are added or removed and how those changes affect order and size. After partner discussion, invite a few students to share out with the class.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "57a0c108-c341-48dc-ac2a-a29421e4e429",
        "lesson_activity.key": "70098461-e736-4f1a-8fe0-10ee34e985d8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "25df5e1f-495d-43e9-ae45-e07d855f1339",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Prepare students to brainstorm properties for a real-world item.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Place students in pairs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each pair of students a copy of the My First Object activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask pairs to choose one everyday item (suggest backpack, bicycle, or dessert) and write it on their template. Remind them to avoid personal or identity-based examples (like specific people) to keep the activity comfortable for everyone. Have pairs list three to four properties and example values for their chosen item on the template. Encourage them to think about what someone would need to know to understand or choose this item.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** If students are stuck, ask prompts like: What does it look like? How do you use it? Where would you see it? Keep examples concrete and non-sensitive.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students suggest using a person, gently redirect them toward non-person items and explain that you will talk about fairness and representation without using classmates or personal identities as examples."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "25df5e1f-495d-43e9-ae45-e07d855f1339",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ec147544-79da-4ac1-8000-8650219846d5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "86f24ff8-b7c6-4bf1-9975-970d9fe018c7",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "86f24ff8-b7c6-4bf1-9975-970d9fe018c7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ec147544-79da-4ac1-8000-8650219846d5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "74dc4876-fafa-4906-8a39-e881d3090d5a",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts:\n- *If AI created a “home” object, how might it represent it differently?*\n- *What might it get wrong or oversimplify?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students understand that AI-generated “properties” are not neutral and they reflect patterns and assumptions from data. AI might default to properties like “has a backyard,” “multiple bedrooms,” or “owned by a family,” while missing realities like apartments, shared spaces, multigenerational households, or temporary housing. Emphasize that these gaps show how bias can appear in the properties chosen, and this is why fairness and clarity matter when we represent real-world data in code.\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "74dc4876-fafa-4906-8a39-e881d3090d5a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ec147544-79da-4ac1-8000-8650219846d5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "74764428-20f6-45ca-8040-98627360b942",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Emphasize that AI often relies on common patterns, which can lead to missing or oversimplified representations. Connect back to student examples (e.g., “home”) and reinforce that developers are responsible for reviewing and improving AI-generated data.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "74764428-20f6-45ca-8040-98627360b942",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ec147544-79da-4ac1-8000-8650219846d5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0b725d7b-1355-4449-aaa3-2968deb8bfdc",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Bridge from the informal brainstorm to the formal coding idea of objects.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Briefly introduce the idea of an object as a way to group related information about one thing. Keep the explanation short and connect it to the warm-up activity, emphasizing that students will explore how this works in code next.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define property. Point out that each label around the backpack represents one property — a named fact about the object. Explain that properties help the computer store and work with specific details, like color or size, and that these facts can be read or updated in code.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 4, Level 1 to explore the object in code and observe how information is stored and accessed. Encourage them to identify each property and what value it holds. Ask students to notice how all the data for one item is grouped together and how this compares to using arrays.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students identifying how the backpack object stores data. Make sure students understand how the code accesses properties (e.g., backpack.color). Ask: “How would accessing this data be different if the data were stored in arrays?”",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Creating an Object",
        "progression_name": "Explore an Object"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0b725d7b-1355-4449-aaa3-2968deb8bfdc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4b1bc73b-ddb2-42e4-acf9-7e61adaa6a49"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "03900dba-1c15-4dd3-be2e-1ad00691f15a",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *After exploring, why might we choose an object instead of parallel arrays?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to compare objects and arrays based on what they observed. Students should recognize that objects group related data together in one place, while arrays rely on positions across multiple lists. Emphasize that objects reduce the risk of data getting out of sync and make programs easier to read and manage.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "03900dba-1c15-4dd3-be2e-1ad00691f15a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4b1bc73b-ddb2-42e4-acf9-7e61adaa6a49"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7573557f-97aa-46fb-8467-2c39be174842",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animation to explain how to create an object.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain when to use dot notation to access and update a value stored in an object. Highlight that there is a dot after the object name (“item1”) before the property (“food”) is called.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that dot notation is great when you know the property name ahead of time, while bracket notation is helpful when the property name comes from a variable or has special characters. Show the two short examples and connect them to the previous example.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7573557f-97aa-46fb-8467-2c39be174842",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4b1bc73b-ddb2-42e4-acf9-7e61adaa6a49"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dc9c6783-1b7c-4dcc-9501-f5ac5bd422bd",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In the following levels, you will use AI to help you practice both dot notation and bracket notation.\n",
        "progression_name": "Reflection: Human vs AI Object Design",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dc9c6783-1b7c-4dcc-9501-f5ac5bd422bd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4b1bc73b-ddb2-42e4-acf9-7e61adaa6a49"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6e55aaba-2aec-46fc-9fc9-83eee6c05131",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Before students generate objects with AI, use this slide to introduce a simple routine for evaluating AI-generated objects. Walk through each question and connect it to their work, emphasizing that students should check whether the properties match their purpose, whether anything is missing, and whether the object reflects realistic and fair representations.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6e55aaba-2aec-46fc-9fc9-83eee6c05131",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4b1bc73b-ddb2-42e4-acf9-7e61adaa6a49"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "59bdd9f3-f919-4f8d-a508-388832dfdfdd",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Give students multiple opportunities to practice creating an object and accessing and updating object properties.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 4, Level 2 to complete Levels 2 through 6.\n- Level 2: Students use AI Tutor to generate an object from their warm-up properties.\n- Level 3: Students complete a Check For Understanding question.\n- Level 4: Students access a property of their object.\n- Level 5: Update an object using dot and bracket notation.\n- Level 6: Update an object with user input\n- Level 7: Change at least two existing property values using dot notation or bracket notation, add at least one new property to the object, and modify the HTML and render() so all updated and new properties appear on screen.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for:\n- Objects with three to five properties using correct { key: value } syntax.\n- Students identifying and revising any unclear or biased properties or values from the AI.\n- Students successfully displaying the object via innerText or console.log.\n\nAsk probing questions like: What did the AI assume about this property? Does that feel fair or accurate? What did you decide to change and why?",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Create and Update Objects",
        "progression_name": "Create and Update Objects",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that AI is a helper, not the boss. Encourage them to keep objects simple and neutral, and to write respectful comments when critiquing AI choices (for example, “The AI focused on X, but I changed it to Y because…”).\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use the reflection in Level 3 to check whether students understand both object structure and their ongoing responsibility when using AI to represent real-world data.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "59bdd9f3-f919-4f8d-a508-388832dfdfdd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4b1bc73b-ddb2-42e4-acf9-7e61adaa6a49"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f8b04deb-22c9-4d37-99b8-bde11b25cf2b",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f8b04deb-22c9-4d37-99b8-bde11b25cf2b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4b1bc73b-ddb2-42e4-acf9-7e61adaa6a49"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7014b835-b38b-438e-b100-c05619bee24c",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students turn and talk with a partner to share their ideas. Encourage them to explain how using objects helps represent real-world data clearly and fairly by describing how properties group related information and how this avoids issues like missing, mismatched, or biased data. After partner discussion, invite a few students to share out with the class.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary.\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7014b835-b38b-438e-b100-c05619bee24c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2c507a95-3256-46ce-90ba-44c9db8debe5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4209176b-7eb9-4ccc-b532-6f5a100b16d0",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Notice that missing or uneven data changes how information is represented.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What's one thing that seems missing or over-represented?*\n- *What’s one way an AI might get an unfair picture if it trained only on this data?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students connect patterns in the dataset (blanks, repeats, uneven groups) to how information can become unfair or incomplete. Listen for ideas like \"The dataset would ignore \" or \"An AI might think  is the only important group.\" Guide students to link representation in data to fairness.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4209176b-7eb9-4ccc-b532-6f5a100b16d0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9605d9cc-6938-443e-baf9-543992b830f2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6759da44-8512-4beb-8cb2-3f16a341d3e5",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Recognize that a dataset can be represented as an array of objects, where each object has the same structure but different values.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show students the items and have them briefly turn and talk: “What do you notice?” and “What information do all of these include?” Guide them to identify shared fields like name, location, and ID. Do not introduce arrays yet. If students focus only on the objects themselves, prompt them to look at the labels under each item and identify what information is repeated.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Push students’ thinking by asking: “If we had 100 of these, how would we store them?” and “What would need to stay the same?” Give students time to share ideas such as lists or grouping, but do not confirm or introduce arrays yet. Ask students “How would you make sure the same kinds of information stay connected for each item?”\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk students through the code by connecting it to the previous question about storing many items. Focus on one object and explain that everything inside one set of curly braces represents one item. Then point out that each key-value pair (id, name, location) is a property that describes that item. Emphasize that this structure lets us store many similar items together while keeping their information organized. Have students annotate their Unit Guide by labeling the object and identifying its properties.\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Understanding Arrays of Objects",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may think the entire code block is one object. Have them point to a single { ... } and identify it as one item, then identify the key-value pairs inside as properties.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6759da44-8512-4beb-8cb2-3f16a341d3e5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8facf6a2-3841-493a-9906-4d2071606780"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "125e7d33-d217-4906-b73a-a5f249d4fa16",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Build on the previous slide by connecting one object to many. Explain that while each set of { } represents one item, the square brackets [ ] group all of those items together into a single list. Emphasize that this list (the array) represents the full dataset. Click through the animation to highlight the brackets, then have students annotate their Unit Guide by identifying the array as the full dataset.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may focus on individual objects and overlook the brackets. Emphasize that the square brackets group all items together into one dataset.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "125e7d33-d217-4906-b73a-a5f249d4fa16",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8facf6a2-3841-493a-9906-4d2071606780"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7f773f12-be48-408c-bddb-8e8b351d7fde",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Generate a dataset using AI and identify patterns such as missing, overrepresented, or uneven data.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 5, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 and 2. Have students pair up with their elbow partner so that they can share and compare the objects AI adds to their datasets and discuss patterns they notice. Make sure both partners choose the same topic: animal adoption, community events, or transit stops.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students successfully generate a dataset and are inspecting it rather than just moving on. Ask:\n- What patterns do you notice in the data?\n- Which items show up the most?\n- Is anything missing or less detailed?",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Exploring Patterns in a Dataset",
        "progression_name": "Explore Lists of Objects",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may think the entire dataset is one object. Have them point to a single { ... } and identify it as one item, then trace the surrounding [ ] as the full dataset.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7f773f12-be48-408c-bddb-8e8b351d7fde",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8facf6a2-3841-493a-9906-4d2071606780"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5c985cf6-e746-4dee-8fe9-707295142b4b",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *Which items were missing data?*\n- *Which items showed up the most?*\n- *What might AI assume from this data?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to identify patterns in the dataset (missing data, repeated values, uneven representation) and explain how those patterns could lead an AI to make incorrect or incomplete assumptions. Emphasize the connection between what is included or missing in the data and what a computer or AI system might conclude.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define bias by connecting it directly to the patterns students just identified. Explain that missing or overrepresented data leads to bias and can cause unfair or incomplete conclusions. Emphasize that doesn’t just happen—it comes from the data we choose and how we structure it, so if the data is uneven, the output will reflect that."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5c985cf6-e746-4dee-8fe9-707295142b4b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8facf6a2-3841-493a-9906-4d2071606780"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "75726b38-bafa-473c-a2cc-6721850ce5dd",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Analyze how patterns in a dataset affect what is displayed and what users or AI systems might assume, and evaluate how these patterns influence fairness and accuracy.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 5, Level 3 to complete Levels 3 and 4. Direct them to continue comparing and discussing with their elbow partners in each level.\n- Level 3: Students compare what the app displays to the full dataset and consider how showing only some data changes what a user might assume.\n- Level 4: Students examine differences in how much information each item includes and reflect on how that affects perception and decision-making.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for whether students are identifying patterns in their dataset and comparing what is shown in the UI to what exists in the full array. Students may assume the dataset is complete because the app works, or focus only on what is displayed instead of checking the underlying data. If students are stuck, prompt them to look for missing or uneven information and compare items directly. Ask:\n- What patterns do you notice in your dataset?\n- Is anything missing or less detailed?\n- Does the app show everything in the dataset?\n- What might someone assume based on on what is shown?\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Analyzing Data Fairness and Impact",
        "progression_name": "Data Fairness",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may focus only on what is displayed in the app and not compare it to the full dataset. Prompt them to look back at the code and identify what is not being shown.\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use student work in Levels 3-4 to assess whether they can identify missing or overrepresented data, explain how those patterns affect what is displayed, and connect those differences to what a user or AI system might assume. Look for students who use specific examples from their dataset to justify their thinking.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "75726b38-bafa-473c-a2cc-6721850ce5dd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8facf6a2-3841-493a-9906-4d2071606780"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "399c1855-560a-474a-a3c0-4a37a906c27a",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Group students based on the topic they chose for Level 3 (Animal Adoption, Community Events, or Metro Expansion). Have pairs join another pair to compare their datasets. Prompt them to identify what data is missing or overrepresented and explain how those differences affect what is shown and what a user or AI might assume from the data. Encourage them to decide which dataset would lead to a more accurate or fair result before moving into the class discussion.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Before the lesson, plan how students will move into groups of four. If space is tight, designate group areas ahead of time. Be prepared to support uneven group sizes so all students can participate meaningfully.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "399c1855-560a-474a-a3c0-4a37a906c27a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8facf6a2-3841-493a-9906-4d2071606780"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "03ac2568-fd98-4219-9250-3bb75fd13efa",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "03ac2568-fd98-4219-9250-3bb75fd13efa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8facf6a2-3841-493a-9906-4d2071606780"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b3088f70-6939-4545-8b10-d8b5fc81b28b",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What differences did you notice between your dataset and the other group’s?*\n- *What data was missing or overrepresented, and how did that change what the UI showed and what a user or AI might assume?*\n- *Which dataset would lead to a more accurate or fair result? Why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students compare datasets to identify missing or overrepresented data and explain how these differences change what is shown and what a user or AI might assume. They evaluate which dataset leads to a more accurate or fair result and consider what changes would improve the dataset.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b3088f70-6939-4545-8b10-d8b5fc81b28b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8facf6a2-3841-493a-9906-4d2071606780"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6e926283-6352-4887-bf6a-ce038cf641a8",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 5, Level 5 to respond to the Check for Understanding questions. Remind them to use specific examples from their dataset to explain what patterns they noticed and what an AI system might assume.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for whether students are identifying patterns in their dataset and explaining how those patterns affect what a user or AI system might assume. Students may describe patterns without connecting them to outcomes or rely on general statements instead of specific examples. Prompt them to use evidence from their dataset and make their reasoning explicit.\n",
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use student responses as an exit ticket to assess whether they can identify missing or overrepresented data, explain how those patterns influence what is shown, and describe what an AI system might assume or get wrong based on the dataset.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6e926283-6352-4887-bf6a-ce038cf641a8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8facf6a2-3841-493a-9906-4d2071606780"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "013258fc-c0e4-4f3b-890e-17fdf1d34fd7",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "013258fc-c0e4-4f3b-890e-17fdf1d34fd7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8facf6a2-3841-493a-9906-4d2071606780"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9e294197-af86-42f3-8b8e-1032ffddbaa0",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students turn and talk with a partner to share their ideas. Encourage them to explain how the way data is structured and what is included or missing in a dataset can affect what users or AI systems learn. Prompt students to use examples from their dataset or the activity to support their thinking. After partner discussion, invite a few students to share out with the class.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9e294197-af86-42f3-8b8e-1032ffddbaa0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "04359687-2d2b-4efb-9a4a-846b4ac6f381"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e842302d-264c-4556-abd0-ced796f8bf96",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "So if the data has patterns or gaps, that affects what an AI learns. Now imagine what happens when a system applies those patterns over and over…\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e842302d-264c-4556-abd0-ced796f8bf96",
        "lesson_activity.key": "04359687-2d2b-4efb-9a4a-846b4ac6f381"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5612a32a-107f-4013-825c-cb14828ffcfd",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5612a32a-107f-4013-825c-cb14828ffcfd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "04359687-2d2b-4efb-9a4a-846b4ac6f381"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1f4cbc4e-5e21-45ec-b643-09588fbfc93c",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Help students notice that many apps systematically transform data from one form to another.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students get into a small group (three to five students). The group should select one of the data transformation examples presented here :\n- A camera filter that turns every photo black-and-white.\n- A playlist app that adds a heart icon to every liked song.\n- A delivery app that converts street names into map pins.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What kind of hidden process occurs to transform the original data into these new displays?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to the idea that each input item goes through the same rule or process to create a new version (for example, every pixel becomes grayscale, every liked song gets a heart icon, every address turns into a pin). Clarify that the original data often stays stored while a new version is created to be displayed."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1f4cbc4e-5e21-45ec-b643-09588fbfc93c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e245eda6-e1bf-42d0-8f8c-08b40592bb00"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "321dfa84-b2c9-4764-b6dc-d89911cd4aa7",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In each of these examples, the same rule was applied to every item just as .map() applied that behavior in code. In today’s lesson, we’ll continue using the .map() method to display our objects.\n",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Use a timer or have students pass a talking object to keep the flow moving. Works well to build quick consensus or identify patterns in responses.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "321dfa84-b2c9-4764-b6dc-d89911cd4aa7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e245eda6-e1bf-42d0-8f8c-08b40592bb00"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d91d6e64-e092-4198-9fe9-f910236e3820",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d91d6e64-e092-4198-9fe9-f910236e3820",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e245eda6-e1bf-42d0-8f8c-08b40592bb00"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "37d07485-4225-4a57-b9c9-7511b6ea1081",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Quickly refresh students’ understanding of how .map() applies the same rule to every item in a list before they use it.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students what they remember about .map() from Lesson 2. Click through the animation and briefly review how .map() applies the same rule to each item in a list. Use the “factory machine” analogy to reinforce that one rule is repeated for every item, then transition quickly to the exploration activity. Emphasize that the rule inside the “machine” can come from a programmer or an AI tool, and whatever rule is used will be applied to every item.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Emphasize that the original list is not changed—.map() creates a new version instead.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "37d07485-4225-4a57-b9c9-7511b6ea1081",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5526d7f4-608b-4e55-ab79-fca6f06d802b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "63a15305-7673-4d8d-a95a-3f0386f9b050",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 6, Level 1. Explain that they should explore how the cards are created and look for patterns in how the data is being used.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students noticing that multiple cards are created using the same structure. Prompt them with:\n- How many cards are being created?\n- What is changing in each card?\n- What is staying the same?\n- What part of the code do you think is responsible for creating all the cards?\n",
        "progression_name": "Exploring .map()"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "63a15305-7673-4d8d-a95a-3f0386f9b050",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5526d7f4-608b-4e55-ab79-fca6f06d802b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4a9109e8-a31e-4a5b-ba15-c88a3f31c879",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Bring the class back together and connect this code to what students just observed in the app. Walk through how one object is turned into one card, and emphasize that .map() repeats this same process for every object in the list.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, ask students “Which part of the object is used to build each part of the card?” This helps students connect job.role to role, job.loation to location and job.pay to pay\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4a9109e8-a31e-4a5b-ba15-c88a3f31c879",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5526d7f4-608b-4e55-ab79-fca6f06d802b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9917e5bd-e61f-41c8-a7ce-254dfaf6ab57",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9917e5bd-e61f-41c8-a7ce-254dfaf6ab57",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5526d7f4-608b-4e55-ab79-fca6f06d802b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "99cab65c-d169-4fff-8e24-9460020fc641",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *If the rule inside the .map() “machine” is wrong, what happens to every item?*\n- *If an AI generated that rule, what kinds of mistakes might it repeat across the entire dataset?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to recognize that .map() applies the same rule to every item, so a flawed rule will produce the same mistake across all outputs. Emphasize that this is powerful but risky—especially when the rule is generated by AI—because one mistake can scale across an entire dataset.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Why might it be risky to ask AI to build a whole app itself?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to recognize that when AI generates a whole feature or app, a single flawed rule can create repeated errors across many items or users. Emphasize that humans need to review, test, and guide AI-generated code to prevent these kinds of issues.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "99cab65c-d169-4fff-8e24-9460020fc641",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5526d7f4-608b-4e55-ab79-fca6f06d802b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9bd5ad8d-cd6a-4615-9155-3f6646c0fc61",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Reinforce that to build a complex app, students shouldn’t ask AI to “build everything” at once. Instead, they should break the problem into smaller, manageable steps. Connect this to their task by having them identify what information each card should display and how it should be organized before writing a prompt.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through the example prompt and highlight the three parts—goals, constraints, and edge cases. Remind students how each part helps make the AI output more accurate and consistent. Encourage students to use this structure when writing their own prompts before returning to the activity.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "This is framework that students have used throughout the course. It is helpful for decomposing a larger problem into smaller steps, then creating targeted and focused prompts to implement one feature at a time. Encourage them to draft their prompt before asking AI for code. Students can plan out this prompt in a new file and save it to their backpack to use in the future.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9bd5ad8d-cd6a-4615-9155-3f6646c0fc61",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5526d7f4-608b-4e55-ab79-fca6f06d802b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b72c98fb-aca6-4d21-992a-591079a39dcf",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b72c98fb-aca6-4d21-992a-591079a39dcf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5526d7f4-608b-4e55-ab79-fca6f06d802b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "50f2aa4a-8cfd-4062-adcb-41d472e375c6",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 6, Level 2 to complete Levels 2 through 5. Remind them to use their plan and prompt structure to build and render their cards step by step.\n- Level 2: Students us AI Tutor to render cards on the page\n- Level 3: Students use .map() to render an array of objects as multiple items.\n- Level 4: Students add button actions to update their array and re-render the list.\n- Level 5: Students complete a Check For Understanding question.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for evidence that students are drafting prompts in the markdown files before asking AI for code. Support students in explicitly naming the steps, like \"I need to map the data to a card\" rather than just guessing. Assist students who might be struggling by having them display just one property at a time. Support them in choosing which property to display and drafting a specific prompt for AI. Ask: “What rule is your code applying to each item?” or  “If that rule were wrong, how would you notice?”\n",
        "progression_name": "Rendering Lists of Objects"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "50f2aa4a-8cfd-4062-adcb-41d472e375c6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5526d7f4-608b-4e55-ab79-fca6f06d802b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d360db13-6cf4-4fd8-ab1e-87a12223f739",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Use this slide to help students make sense of what they just built. Walk through the diagram at a high level, connecting each step to their experience: starting with data (an array of objects), using .map() to create a card for each item, combining the results, and displaying them on the page. Keep the explanation brief and focus on the overall process rather than specific code details.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d360db13-6cf4-4fd8-ab1e-87a12223f739",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5526d7f4-608b-4e55-ab79-fca6f06d802b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "71353f18-3520-4f2b-9e51-5d71c1a9f378",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Where have you seen a process like this used in real apps?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to recognize that many apps apply the same rule or process to multiple pieces of data to generate consistent displays. Look for examples such as:\n- A social media feed where each post is displayed using the same layout\n- A shopping site where each product appears as a card with image, title, and price\n- A playlist where each song is shown in the same format\n- A map app where each location becomes a pin\n\nEncourage students to describe what the repeated “rule” might be in their example.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "71353f18-3520-4f2b-9e51-5d71c1a9f378",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5526d7f4-608b-4e55-ab79-fca6f06d802b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d04d02a8-1661-435b-a46f-ee36283a2c85",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What changes did you have to make to your prompts after you reviewed AI’s output?*\n- *What parts of your prompt would stay the same no matter what data you were working with?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to reflect on how they revised their prompts to improve AI’s output and identify which parts of their prompts can be reused across different contexts. Students should recognize that clear, structured prompts lead to more accurate and consistent results, especially when the same logic is applied across many items.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Use students’ responses from the previous discussion to highlight which parts of their prompts stayed the same across contexts. Explain that these shared elements can be turned into a reusable pattern, or abstraction, that can be applied in future tasks.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students struggle with the abstraction, encourage them to ask the AI to \"explain this code in one sentence\". Often, the AI's explanation will already be abstract (e.g., \"This function takes an object and maps it...\"), which students can use as inspiration for their generalized prompt.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d04d02a8-1661-435b-a46f-ee36283a2c85",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5526d7f4-608b-4e55-ab79-fca6f06d802b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "40b1303a-813a-43d5-b46d-a8aad4d44dfb",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "40b1303a-813a-43d5-b46d-a8aad4d44dfb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5526d7f4-608b-4e55-ab79-fca6f06d802b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d5b2894e-ebb4-4b5e-943c-36e6ea4572b0",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d5b2894e-ebb4-4b5e-943c-36e6ea4572b0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4ae1627b-f50d-492c-97a4-7d65e7ed8ef2"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "128a7684-893d-498c-9321-6d51830b3c75",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Surface that different people make different choices—even with the same prompt—based on what they prioritize.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students about 30–60 seconds to write their top 3. Then have them turn and talk with a partner to compare their lists and explain why they chose their items.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may assume there is a “correct” answer. Emphasize that there is no single best list—different choices reflect different priorities.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "128a7684-893d-498c-9321-6d51830b3c75",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8824978e-4781-4610-9d82-787f9855dfa4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3ff5b1ac-e5f2-4312-84a9-45635ca5ca34",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3ff5b1ac-e5f2-4312-84a9-45635ca5ca34",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8824978e-4781-4610-9d82-787f9855dfa4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8c1cb310-c4bc-4a13-af0c-8254d13e1bfc",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What was different about your lists?*\n- *Why did you choose different items?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that different people make different choices—even with the same prompt—based on what they prioritize. They identify the criteria they used (such as cost, interest, or convenience) and begin to understand that these choices act like rules that lead to different results and highlight some options while leaving others out.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8c1cb310-c4bc-4a13-af0c-8254d13e1bfc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8824978e-4781-4610-9d82-787f9855dfa4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d5b2fb74-87eb-4ed4-9997-13f1e4fdee44",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "This is what product designers and engineers do—decide what to show and what to prioritize. AI systems make similar decisions based on patterns in the data they are given. Today, we’ll explore how those choices affect fairness.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d5b2fb74-87eb-4ed4-9997-13f1e4fdee44",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8824978e-4781-4610-9d82-787f9855dfa4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e96fda87-7ef2-48bc-85bd-d068a6ad4f11",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e96fda87-7ef2-48bc-85bd-d068a6ad4f11",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8824978e-4781-4610-9d82-787f9855dfa4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "72188695-9a1c-45d7-a3c4-c5168f056a1b",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Explore how display choices—what is included, excluded, and emphasized—shape what users see and can lead to biased or incomplete interpretations of the data.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Put students into groups of 2-3.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each group a copy of the Display Audit Challenge activity guide, a set of display cards, one display goal, and a larger piece of poster paper to serve as their “web app display”.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Providing each group with a piece of poster paper is optional, but it can help students better simulate how a web app displays information. Students can physically arrange their cards on the paper as a “display,” and after revising, they can tape or glue their final selections and add a brief explanation of what they changed and why. This also allows you to display group work around the room for comparison and discussion.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "72188695-9a1c-45d7-a3c4-c5168f056a1b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4ebc0c28-afba-4e67-a02c-ae5f7fce48d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "924fbef1-c42c-40dc-80bf-a0ea3055909b",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "924fbef1-c42c-40dc-80bf-a0ea3055909b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4ebc0c28-afba-4e67-a02c-ae5f7fce48d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "438122f3-59ff-45a9-a747-c426a654c653",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the activity by framing students as designers. Explain that all groups will use the same dataset, but may create different displays based on their decisions about what to show, how to order it, and what to highlight.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Resist the urge to explain bias or fairness here. Let students experience differences in their displays first—this will make the later discussion more meaningful.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "438122f3-59ff-45a9-a747-c426a654c653",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4ebc0c28-afba-4e67-a02c-ae5f7fce48d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "080681c5-0c20-4956-bd86-2d1dfd7566d3",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "080681c5-0c20-4956-bd86-2d1dfd7566d3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4ebc0c28-afba-4e67-a02c-ae5f7fce48d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4a0b38e0-77d7-4f96-9c79-0f38eec7c506",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to begin Part 1 of the activity. Remind them to use their Display Goal to guide their decisions and that they can only choose 5 cards. Encourage them to physically move and arrange the cards on their poster paper before writing anything down.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for whether students are using their display goal to guide their choices and discussing tradeoffs as they select and order cards. Students may choose based on personal preference rather than the goal, or rush to pick cards without comparing options. Prompt them to explain their reasoning and point to specific properties on the cards. Ask:\n- Why did you include this card?\n- How does this fit your display goal?\n- What made you put this one at the top?\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may try to pick cards quickly and move on. Encourage them to pause and compare options—this is where the important thinking happens. Remind them that they will need to explain their choices later.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4a0b38e0-77d7-4f96-9c79-0f38eec7c506",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4ebc0c28-afba-4e67-a02c-ae5f7fce48d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "17cb6ea6-5280-4698-a95c-7f58174bdea9",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "17cb6ea6-5280-4698-a95c-7f58174bdea9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4ebc0c28-afba-4e67-a02c-ae5f7fce48d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7126b104-d09f-4c7e-9c0e-ea838e89eb19",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to leave their Activity Guide with their display (poster paper and selected cards), then move to another group’s display. Explain that they will complete Part 2 on the other group’s Activity Guide, not their own. Remind students to read the group’s Display Goal before analyzing their choices.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for whether students are analyzing the display based on the goal and identifying patterns in what was included or left out. Students may default to personal opinions or forget to use the other group’s Activity Guide. Prompt them to reference the display goal and specific card properties. Ask:\n- What patterns do you notice in what they chose?\n- How does their display goal affect these choices?\n- What would you change about this display?\n- Which items do you think are missing?\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may rush through this step or give surface-level feedback. Encourage them to slow down and compare multiple cards, not just react to one or two. Remind them that their observations will be used by the original group to revise their display.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7126b104-d09f-4c7e-9c0e-ea838e89eb19",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4ebc0c28-afba-4e67-a02c-ae5f7fce48d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d02aaec8-6fb8-46cb-bf93-c31cc1b9d43f",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d02aaec8-6fb8-46cb-bf93-c31cc1b9d43f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4ebc0c28-afba-4e67-a02c-ae5f7fce48d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "29a10757-0caf-48c1-bb7a-a31e36bd0911",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to return to their original display and read the feedback left on their Activity Guide. Have them discuss what they want to change and why, then update their display by adjusting their selected cards, order, or highlighted information. Remind them to complete Part 3 on their Activity Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for whether students are using the feedback to make meaningful changes rather than small or random adjustments. Students may resist changing their original choices or make changes without clear reasoning. Prompt them to connect their revision to the feedback they received. Ask:\n- What did the other group notice about your display?\n- What change are you making based on that feedback?\n- How does your change improve your display?\n- What would a user see differently now?\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may try to defend their original display instead of revising it. Encourage them to treat feedback as a way to improve their design, not as something to argue against. Remind them that even small changes can lead to different outcomes.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "29a10757-0caf-48c1-bb7a-a31e36bd0911",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4ebc0c28-afba-4e67-a02c-ae5f7fce48d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9aca9f9c-20c7-4a12-800e-bc4ea0606787",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9aca9f9c-20c7-4a12-800e-bc4ea0606787",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4ebc0c28-afba-4e67-a02c-ae5f7fce48d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e0fb5765-628d-4e63-b663-8b3268856166",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *Even though everyone used the same data, how might an AI system trained on this display or dataset make similar assumptions?*\n- *What kinds of events or people were most likely to be left out across different displays?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to recognize that even when working with the same data, different display choices (what is included, excluded, or emphasized) can lead to different results and interpretations. Students should identify patterns in what was shown and left out, and explain how those choices could shape what a user or AI system might see, focus on, or assume about the data.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e0fb5765-628d-4e63-b663-8b3268856166",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4ebc0c28-afba-4e67-a02c-ae5f7fce48d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "621295ed-610e-4ee8-a16b-55793500aa68",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 7, Level 1 to complete the check for understanding questions.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for whether students are connecting their display choices to what was shown, what was left out, and how that affects what a user might think. Students may describe what they did without explaining the impact. Prompt them to use specific examples from their dataset and reasoning from the activity.\n",
        "progression_name": "Check Your Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may give general or opinion-based answers. Encourage them to reference specific cards, fields, or changes from their display to support their reasoning.\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use student responses from the activity guide and level 1 to formatively assess whether they can identify what was included or left out in a display, explain how those choices affect user interpretation, and describe how different rules or criteria could lead to different outcomes.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "621295ed-610e-4ee8-a16b-55793500aa68",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4ebc0c28-afba-4e67-a02c-ae5f7fce48d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "35a03243-b1ef-428f-97ba-c0cebdc5d6f8",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "35a03243-b1ef-428f-97ba-c0cebdc5d6f8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4ebc0c28-afba-4e67-a02c-ae5f7fce48d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "50220519-399e-46dc-b418-7adb0ae8242f",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students turn and talk with a partner to share their ideas. Encourage them to use examples from their display, the audit, and their revisions to explain how different choices affected what was shown and what was left out. After partner discussion, invite a few students to share out with the class.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "50220519-399e-46dc-b418-7adb0ae8242f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9a03f06f-c7c1-4af0-a509-93c8cad35d00"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6e9eb974-fd19-4408-b8e1-ea4065657b59",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** ecognize that filters are rules (conditions) that determine what is visible, and that every filter rule carries assumptions and consequences.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students compare the two filters. Ask “What kinds of books show up in Version A vs Version B?”,  “What patterns do you notice?” and “What rule do you think is being used to decide what shows up?” Explain that each version is based on a rule, and AI systems use similar rules to decide what gets shown or recommended.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Version A:\n- Animal Farm, George Orwell\n- The Great Gatsby, F. Scott Fitzgerald\n- Pride and Prejudice, Jane Austen\n- The Hobbit, J.R.R. Tolkien\n\nVersion B:\n- The Summer I Turned Pretty, Jenny Han\n- Sunrise on the Reaping, Suzanne Collins\n- Divergent, Veronica Roth\n- Dune, Frank Herbert"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6e9eb974-fd19-4408-b8e1-ea4065657b59",
        "lesson_activity.key": "81b41a47-019e-4bba-9611-d4b4ffe383b5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b3ba5eac-c139-45ae-a112-aad9ec7bfde4",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b3ba5eac-c139-45ae-a112-aad9ec7bfde4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "81b41a47-019e-4bba-9611-d4b4ffe383b5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0e50af26-dfad-4b9d-8984-cfa01706cc52",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What assumptions did AI make?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to identify the assumptions behind each condition and explain how those assumptions define what counts as “Top Rated” or “Trending.” Help them recognize that cutoff values (like > 4.5 or > 100) are decisions that can shape what is included, excluded, or emphasized.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0e50af26-dfad-4b9d-8984-cfa01706cc52",
        "lesson_activity.key": "81b41a47-019e-4bba-9611-d4b4ffe383b5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "39fc59f1-771a-48f4-b617-8b690a682536",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What assumption does a condition like popularity > 75 make about what counts as ‘popular’?*\n- *What items might be excluded because of a condition like soldToday > 100?*\n- *If this were your app, how could you make the filter more fair or inclusive?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to recognize that filter conditions encode assumptions about what qualifies to be included, and that those assumptions directly determine which items are included or excluded. These assumptions create consistent patterns in what is shown and what is left out. Students should connect these choices to real impacts on fairness and understand that both humans and AI systems use these kinds of conditions, so these assumptions shape what is included, excluded, or emphasized in the output.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may struggle to identify what gets excluded by a condition. Prompt them with examples such as:\n- Older classics (e.g., Pride and Prejudice, The Great Gatsby) that aren’t selling in high daily volumes\n- New releases that haven’t had time to gain traction yet\n- Less mainstream books like poetry, lesser-known authors, or specialized topics\n\nYou can also ask: “Which types of books would never meet this condition?” to help students recognize patterns of systematic exclusion.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "39fc59f1-771a-48f4-b617-8b690a682536",
        "lesson_activity.key": "81b41a47-019e-4bba-9611-d4b4ffe383b5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "aeb1d7a9-3e50-4a5d-987c-cac32589cb34",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "aeb1d7a9-3e50-4a5d-987c-cac32589cb34",
        "lesson_activity.key": "81b41a47-019e-4bba-9611-d4b4ffe383b5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "00318cb4-48e5-4428-8847-aa279f945b5d",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Wrap up the warm-up by stating explicitly that every filter starts as a condition, and every condition is a choice that changes what is visible and what is hidden. Connect this to prior learning: just like with .map(), this condition is a rule—but instead of changing every item, it decides which items are included or excluded. Optionally, if you have one extra minute, invite students to jot a quick pseudocode condition such as “if book.popularity > 4.5” in their notebook.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students write pseudocode, remind them it does not need perfect syntax. Focus them on the idea that a one-line rule can have a big impact on who gets included or excluded.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "00318cb4-48e5-4428-8847-aa279f945b5d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "81b41a47-019e-4bba-9611-d4b4ffe383b5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8ae9046a-f3d4-4866-9308-e49291b25566",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8ae9046a-f3d4-4866-9308-e49291b25566",
        "lesson_activity.key": "81b41a47-019e-4bba-9611-d4b4ffe383b5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2bca86b4-8b3f-4b89-806c-e2d2198ca8f6",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2bca86b4-8b3f-4b89-806c-e2d2198ca8f6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "81b41a47-019e-4bba-9611-d4b4ffe383b5"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7ad62f54-e65b-4c9f-8a5b-fb7b02e2a9e8",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Discover how different filter conditions affect which items are included or excluded.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Explore Filters"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7ad62f54-e65b-4c9f-8a5b-fb7b02e2a9e8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2009b77f-aba7-4f62-853b-b435fbd2524e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ad71ad94-272c-48ee-a71e-6740e6f1c457",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In this part of the lesson, AI is your code coach. You will not ask it to build the whole app—only the filter functions. Your job is to understand the rules being used and how they affect what gets included or excluded.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ad71ad94-272c-48ee-a71e-6740e6f1c457",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2009b77f-aba7-4f62-853b-b435fbd2524e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e2e3fb11-27d4-46ee-baf3-3577a6766332",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Lesson 8, Level 1.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students copying and pasting AI’s explanation without reviewing it. Check that:\n- Students are testing out each filter and can explain it in their own words.\n- Students understand that .map() is being used for iteration here.\n- Students can identify a good practice like avoiding unnecessary undefined values or keeping output arrays clean and predictable.\n",
        "progression_name": "Explore Filtering",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Emphasize that students should read AI's explanations of the code and put it into their own words when adding comments."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e2e3fb11-27d4-46ee-baf3-3577a6766332",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2009b77f-aba7-4f62-853b-b435fbd2524e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "499475f7-9e82-45fc-8c9e-82e1a2a2a48a",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "499475f7-9e82-45fc-8c9e-82e1a2a2a48a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2009b77f-aba7-4f62-853b-b435fbd2524e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1ed366cc-cc19-4688-92f6-b23c91b419bf",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *What are the two main steps every filter function in this code takes?*\n- *How can we test whether a filter is fair?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to recognize the common structure of every filter function—loop through items, check a condition, and include matching results—and to understand that the condition determines what is included or excluded. Help students connect this to fairness by considering how different conditions can unintentionally exclude valid items and why filters should be tested carefully.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1ed366cc-cc19-4688-92f6-b23c91b419bf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2009b77f-aba7-4f62-853b-b435fbd2524e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "50b050e8-716e-4038-85dd-26681e32fb0f",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce this as a quick way to check whether a filter is working as intended. Connect it to the previous discussion by explaining that instead of guessing whether something is fair, we can use these questions to evaluate the filter more systematically. Emphasize that both humans and AI systems use these kinds of conditions, so this process helps us check the decisions behind what gets included or excluded.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "50b050e8-716e-4038-85dd-26681e32fb0f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2009b77f-aba7-4f62-853b-b435fbd2524e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "41af3830-2467-45c9-aa99-b472927802e7",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Define a filter and understand how it shows up in code.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define filter by connecting it to what students just explored. Explain that a filter is a condition, or rule, that decides which items are included or excluded from a dataset. Emphasize that this rule directly affects what users see and what gets left out.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to walk through how a filter function works. Connect this back to the previous discussion by explaining that this condition is the rule that decides which items are included or excluded, and that changing the condition would change what shows up.\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Define Filters ",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Check that students understand the code by asking the following questions:\n1. What does the condition check for in the filter function?\n1. What would happen if we didn’t set displaySongs = [] before adding filtered songs?\n1. What songs would be left out because of this condition?\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "41af3830-2467-45c9-aa99-b472927802e7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2009b77f-aba7-4f62-853b-b435fbd2524e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5829c433-b755-46f1-87ce-8ab38098b7b7",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5829c433-b755-46f1-87ce-8ab38098b7b7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2009b77f-aba7-4f62-853b-b435fbd2524e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8e9a47f2-217a-45e7-a9f9-3663f81b43e5",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students will use AI to create their own filters.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the three requirements that students need to include when writing AI prompts. Emphasize that the condition they choose is the rule that determines what gets included or excluded, so they should think carefully about what items they want to include and why before prompting AI.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Create Filters",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "In this case, “Define the Constraints” should be where students explicitly state their filter condition. Make sure that they have a condition in mind before prompting AI. Ask them, “What items do you want to include?” and “What might this condition leave out?”\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8e9a47f2-217a-45e7-a9f9-3663f81b43e5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2009b77f-aba7-4f62-853b-b435fbd2524e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f978ffb0-e905-4205-a68d-dd0aabfd9ce5",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f978ffb0-e905-4205-a68d-dd0aabfd9ce5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2009b77f-aba7-4f62-853b-b435fbd2524e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fec4b2ee-1f44-41ee-a1ba-88b19dfbba33",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain to students that if they are unsure how to design their filter, they can prompt AI to generate two different conditions. Have them compare the options and choose one based on which condition better matches their goal and avoids excluding important items. Encourage students to use the ‘How to Check a Filter’ questions to evaluate each option before choosing.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 8, Level 2 to complete Levels 2 through 6.\n- Level 2: Students transfer the pattern to a Movie Picker app by completing a partially written filterActionMovies() function.\n- Level 3: Students plan another filter to the Movie Picker app, such as Comedy, Recent Movies, or High Rated.\n- Level 4: Students code another filter to the Movie Picker app, such as Comedy, Recent Movies, or High Rated.\n- Level 5: Students test their filter against the movie data and explain which movies pass the condition.\n- Level 6: Students complete a Check For Understanding question.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work:\n- Check that each student has clearly clarified the goal of their filter in words before diving into code.\n- Encourage them to update one condition at a time and ask AI to explain any new lines, especially code that accounts for missing or undefined data.\n- Watch that their apps apply filters correctly (UI updates and accurate result counts).",
        "progression_name": "Build a Filter",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students get stuck writing code, suggest specific follow-ups to ask AI, such as  \"Can you adjust this to work with my movies array?\"  If students get stuck debugging their, have them use AI as support by asking, “Can you give some test data that includes missing and undefined fields?” This keeps the focus on testing and fairness, not on generating fancy code.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fec4b2ee-1f44-41ee-a1ba-88b19dfbba33",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2009b77f-aba7-4f62-853b-b435fbd2524e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bd850f0e-910f-42cf-8e3e-27bffc9b32df",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bd850f0e-910f-42cf-8e3e-27bffc9b32df",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2009b77f-aba7-4f62-853b-b435fbd2524e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6713d2bc-5ad9-4ff0-8c80-124fea6b870a",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts\n- *How did you consider fairness when designing your conditions?*\n- *How did AI help you test or improve your conditions?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Help students connect their filter conditions to real impacts on fairness and visibility by recognizing how their chosen conditions encode assumptions about what should be included or excluded. Encourage them to name concrete examples (e.g., “skipping songs with missing popularity so the app does not crash” or “treating 'Tomato' and 'tomato' the same”) and to see AI as a partner in generating test edge cases, spotting unintended exclusions, and refining their logic. Encourage students to reference the ‘How to Check a Filter’ questions when explaining how they tested their conditions.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, invite students to share one fairness-focused test case they used (for example, missing color field, missing genre, or no matches). You can collect a few on the board as a class-generated checklist for testing filters in future lessons. Highlight that good fairness test cases often involve missing, extreme, or unexpected data. Encourage students to reuse and expand this list whenever they design new filters.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6713d2bc-5ad9-4ff0-8c80-124fea6b870a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2009b77f-aba7-4f62-853b-b435fbd2524e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f4a0b546-0760-4c79-b3f1-3c11754761a3",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "saffasfasfasfasfasf"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f4a0b546-0760-4c79-b3f1-3c11754761a3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2009b77f-aba7-4f62-853b-b435fbd2524e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f6582666-90c8-4279-8986-d9c219099324",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Ask students to use one of the question stems to write a question they have from the lesson that day. Give them time to share their questions with the person next to them. Invite 1–2 volunteers to share their examples with the whole class.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f6582666-90c8-4279-8986-d9c219099324",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f17050f3-4243-4bb2-8b89-47aad429ffdc"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b9656d89-1dad-4944-a581-3434df9f5263",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Surface how search results depend on specific rules and assumptions, and begin noticing cases where those rules lead to unexpected results.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to silently predict what results will return for the search term shown on the following slides. Tell them to pay attention to any results that are surprising or don’t match their expectations, and be ready to explain why the computer might be making those choices.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students jump into coding explanations, redirect them to describe what they notice in the results and how those results compare to what they expected.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b9656d89-1dad-4944-a581-3434df9f5263",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d46ae98f-760f-4182-8ff6-3667e66b3338"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4b216373-2022-41ae-bfd0-ed24f481bde0",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4b216373-2022-41ae-bfd0-ed24f481bde0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d46ae98f-760f-4182-8ff6-3667e66b3338"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7d6203cc-b66e-41ed-a9da-84da7130741d",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students to silently predict which cards will be returned when searching for “Pop” in the Title. Encourage them to be specific and commit to a prediction before seeing the results.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Look closely at 'PoPcorN'. It has the letters P, o, and p but didn't get selected. Why?*\n- *What rule is it using?*\n- *What assumption is the code making about how text should match?”*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to recognize that the search is using an exact, case-sensitive match. Push them to identify that the code is comparing characters exactly (including capitalization), even if that does not match user expectations. Listen for students to describe the rule in their own words (e.g., “it has to match exactly” or “capital letters matter”) and to notice that this behavior can lead to results that feel incorrect to users. This sets up the idea that search behavior depends on specific rules, which may not match how users expect search to work and can affect what results users are able to find. Students should also recognize that the computer is making a literal assumption about how text should match, rather than interpreting it flexibly like a human would. This rule creates a consistent pattern in what results are returned and what is left out.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7d6203cc-b66e-41ed-a9da-84da7130741d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d46ae98f-760f-4182-8ff6-3667e66b3338"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "28540982-2e9e-40f4-8e4a-65d725c202f1",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students to predict which cards, if any, will be returned when the search box is left empty. Encourage them to explain their reasoning before revealing the results.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Why does a computer show ALL results when the search bar is empty?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to recognize that an empty search can return all results because it matches everything in the dataset. Push them to consider why this behavior might actually be useful for users (e.g., showing a full list by default) rather than assuming it is a bug.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Guide students to name the rules they observed during the warm-up. Ask them to share what they noticed before revealing each bullet. Use the slide to confirm and organize their ideas rather than introducing new information.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students need support naming the rules, ask:\n- What rule did we see when we searched for \"Pop\"?\n- What did capitalization do?\n- What happened when the search was empty?\n\nStudents may describe what happened without naming the rule clearly. Push them to generalize (e.g., from “PoPcorN didn’t match” to “capital letters have to match exactly”).\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "28540982-2e9e-40f4-8e4a-65d725c202f1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d46ae98f-760f-4182-8ff6-3667e66b3338"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fe5df76b-f4e0-4cb4-9695-3839ab68dd5c",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fe5df76b-f4e0-4cb4-9695-3839ab68dd5c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d46ae98f-760f-4182-8ff6-3667e66b3338"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1d411af7-43ce-4363-977e-c40d9a2f4cc7",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1d411af7-43ce-4363-977e-c40d9a2f4cc7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d46ae98f-760f-4182-8ff6-3667e66b3338"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "02d03c67-bd62-4a9d-a3fd-083027f3eea4",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Examine a dataset to identify patterns and how search rules match data, then use those patterns to propose search rules and consider the assumptions they make about users.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Searching activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students to scan the dataset and look for patterns. Have them circle or highlight fields that repeat and note any values that look similar but are recorded differently (such as differences in capitalization). Prompt them to think about how these differences might affect what a search returns, and what kind of rule might cause those differences to matter.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ask probing questions like: “What kind of value is this field?”, “Could someone search or filter by it?”, “How might differences like capitalization affect what gets returned?” or “What rule would cause that difference?”\n Help students connect patterns in the data to how a search might behave.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Searching Data"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "02d03c67-bd62-4a9d-a3fd-083027f3eea4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5f3e8553-f7b6-4d57-94d5-df98da69ebf0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0a70bd49-8a28-4850-bf42-e04154d7fcb3",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students work individually or in small groups to propose at least two search ideas a user might have for this dataset. For each search, they should identify which field it uses, describe the rule it applies to decide matches, and explain what that rule assumes about what users are looking for. Emphasize that each search idea is based on a rule that determines what data is included or excluded.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ask questions like: “What rule is your search using to decide matches?”, “What does your search assume about what users are looking for?” or “Who might not be able to find what they need using this search?”\nPush students to connect their searches to both rules and impact on users.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students struggle, suggest one search based on a numeric field (e.g., date found) and one based on a categorical field (e.g., category). Encourage them to clearly describe the rule each search is using and what kinds of results it might exclude.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0a70bd49-8a28-4850-bf42-e04154d7fcb3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5f3e8553-f7b6-4d57-94d5-df98da69ebf0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cedd5cc3-5a2f-4d87-87c0-23afec41b9bb",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cedd5cc3-5a2f-4d87-87c0-23afec41b9bb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5f3e8553-f7b6-4d57-94d5-df98da69ebf0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "63a87fd6-849c-41fd-b177-561c4c909c3d",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What rules do you think a search should follow to produce consistent and predictable results?*\n- *What assumptions do your search ideas make—and who might still struggle to find results?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to surface initial ideas about what makes search results consistent and predictable. Push them to propose possible rules (such as matching text or using categories) and to recognize that these rules are based on assumptions about how users search. Help them see that these rules create predictable patterns in what results are returned and can affect who is able to find what they are looking for.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "63a87fd6-849c-41fd-b177-561c4c909c3d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5f3e8553-f7b6-4d57-94d5-df98da69ebf0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "18240740-47d3-40cc-8ff1-a0050ff18501",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *If you type \"Red\" what are you asking the computer to do with that data?*\n- *How does it decide what matches?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to recognize that searching involves checking data against a condition based on the user’s input. Push them to describe that the computer is comparing the search term to values in the dataset using specific rules (like exact matches and capitalization). Students should begin to understand that a search is a process of matching input to data. Guide them to recognize that AI systems use these same kinds of rules to match queries to data, so these assumptions affect what results users see and what they may not be able to find. These rules create consistent patterns in what results are returned and what is left out.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "18240740-47d3-40cc-8ff1-a0050ff18501",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5f3e8553-f7b6-4d57-94d5-df98da69ebf0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4193c1a8-1266-42c5-a84d-08eef83b3124",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define query by connecting it to what students just experienced. Make the connection to if they typed \"Red\" into the search box, that would have been their query. Explain that a query is the input or what you type, that you give the computer so it can find matching results.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may think of a query as a complex or technical concept. Reinforce that a query can be as simple as a single word typed into a search box.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4193c1a8-1266-42c5-a84d-08eef83b3124",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5f3e8553-f7b6-4d57-94d5-df98da69ebf0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "88cf239a-9eb8-4c6e-ac6f-89eedc440e03",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "88cf239a-9eb8-4c6e-ac6f-89eedc440e03",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5f3e8553-f7b6-4d57-94d5-df98da69ebf0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "812b4e2f-e51c-4903-8a9a-b6fd62d2aca2",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Understand how a search function applies rules (like case-insensitivity and empty query behavior) to return matching results, and how those rules affect what results are shown or left out.\n",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Explore and Create a Search"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "812b4e2f-e51c-4903-8a9a-b6fd62d2aca2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5f3e8553-f7b6-4d57-94d5-df98da69ebf0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b6c6a589-2105-4c2e-b87b-d07888a394e3",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Lesson 9, Level 1. Students will:\n- Run the app and test searches like blue, Blue, blu, and an empty query.\n- Use AI to help explain what the search function is doing\n- Add short comments explaining the rules they observe\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students are reading AI explanations and translating them into their own words. Ask: How is this function handling capitalization? What happens when the query is \"\"? Are we changing the original data or returning a new list?\n",
        "progression_name": "Explore Color Search",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students try to “improve” the function, remind them this level is about understanding and explaining the logic, not changing it.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b6c6a589-2105-4c2e-b87b-d07888a394e3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5f3e8553-f7b6-4d57-94d5-df98da69ebf0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7e55cb74-63e6-487d-95ee-05742b8e04e7",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7e55cb74-63e6-487d-95ee-05742b8e04e7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5f3e8553-f7b6-4d57-94d5-df98da69ebf0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "121de772-4517-4649-b933-69a85f0a4b33",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *How did the application handle capitalization differences?*\n- *What rule does it follow when the query is empty?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to recognize that the search function handles capitalization by converting both the search input and the data to lowercase before comparing. Push them to explain how this rule changes matching behavior and creates more consistent results. Help them connect this to user expectations and consider how different rules could affect what results are included or excluded.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to help you point out that both the search input and the product title are converted to lowercase before being compared. Explain that this rule makes the search case-insensitive, which changes how matches are found and creates more consistent results. Emphasize that this affects what results are included or excluded and better aligns with how users expect search to work.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, ask: “What would happen if we only converted one of them?” or “Which searches would fail if we didn’t use this rule?”\nPush students to explain why both sides need to follow the same rule.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "121de772-4517-4649-b933-69a85f0a4b33",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5f3e8553-f7b6-4d57-94d5-df98da69ebf0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "897dc6a4-afe7-4f15-b8a4-cc84b0237859",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "897dc6a4-afe7-4f15-b8a4-cc84b0237859",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5f3e8553-f7b6-4d57-94d5-df98da69ebf0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3aa8b12c-755d-44b5-85a6-fbc0e172429b",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the three search patterns: text field, dropdown, and chip/tag-based search. Connect each pattern to the filters students designed, emphasizing that all three use the same idea of matching data based on a condition, just with different input methods.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3aa8b12c-755d-44b5-85a6-fbc0e172429b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5f3e8553-f7b6-4d57-94d5-df98da69ebf0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "426c489d-0e2c-419f-95d6-0dfd9c0b082e",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "These are common patterns in real apps. Even though they look different, they all work the same way: they match data based on a condition. The only difference is how the user provides the input. That means each one still relies on rules that shape what results are shown and what users are able to find.\n",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "426c489d-0e2c-419f-95d6-0dfd9c0b082e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5f3e8553-f7b6-4d57-94d5-df98da69ebf0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cf4b40c8-345c-4d72-bd01-a50447b56d30",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 9, Level 2 to complete Levels 2 and 3. Explain that they will choose one search style to co-build with AI: Option A (text field), Option B (dropdown), or Option C (chips/tags). Clarify that all options use the same core idea—matching data based on a condition—but differ in how the user provides input.\n- Level 2: Students work with AI to build how they want their search to work\n- Level 3: Students complete a check for understanding\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Help students choose an option that fits their comfort and interest. Encourage them to try different inputs and use AI to suggest additional test cases they might not think of on their own. Ask:\n- What inputs have you tried so far?\n- What did AI suggest you test?\n- Did any of those cases behave differently than expected?\n",
        "progression_name": "Choose Your Search Pattern",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "For students who need more structure, suggest Option B (dropdown). For students excited about arrays and tags, suggest Option C.\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use student work in Level 2-3 to assess whether they can build and explain a working search function, test it with multiple inputs, debug it, and use AI to generate additional test cases. Look for students who use those test cases to identify edge cases and refine how their search behaves.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cf4b40c8-345c-4d72-bd01-a50447b56d30",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5f3e8553-f7b6-4d57-94d5-df98da69ebf0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "72bfff73-0747-495e-8a19-4b84ca6c0669",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "72bfff73-0747-495e-8a19-4b84ca6c0669",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5f3e8553-f7b6-4d57-94d5-df98da69ebf0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "20133777-2a06-4340-a2f7-2a471b4b3a67",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *How did using AI help you come up with test cases or improve your search?*\n- *What is one edge case you want to be sure future searches handle well?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Highlight how students used testing to understand and improve their search functions. Emphasize the importance of edge cases (such as capitalization, partial matches, and empty queries) and how AI can support thinking by suggesting additional test cases students may not have considered. Help students connect these test cases back to the rules they created and how those rules affect what results are returned and what users may or may not be able to find.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "20133777-2a06-4340-a2f7-2a471b4b3a67",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5f3e8553-f7b6-4d57-94d5-df98da69ebf0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "27f1bc47-55c4-4d93-8475-48f5737c1b9c",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students turn and talk with a partner to share their ideas. Encourage them to explain how search functions use rules to match data and how those rules affect what users see or are able to find. Prompt students to use examples from their own search (e.g., how their function handled capitalization, partial matches, or empty queries) to support their thinking. After partner discussion, invite a few students to share out with the class.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "27f1bc47-55c4-4d93-8475-48f5737c1b9c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "27c7d965-4839-46a7-809a-d7ac2c776da0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "03c455fb-da80-4a46-b6a7-41e882935f32",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Notice how different sort rules change what appears first and who gets seen.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Let the gif play to show students three versions of the same feed (Most Popular, Alphabetical, Newest). Give students a moment with each view to jot down which items they see first.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *For each view, which items jump to the top and which are hidden? What assumption does each view make about what matters most?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that the same content can look very different depending on the sort rule. Guide them to notice that certain apps/creators benefit from each rule, and that “Most Popular” might favor already-famous items, “Newest” favors recency, and “Alphabetical” favors those at the beginning of the alphabet. Each view assumes what users care about most, e.g. total downloads, release date, or app name. Emphasize that no sort is neutral."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "03c455fb-da80-4a46-b6a7-41e882935f32",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a4f59a91-34c1-484f-a6e1-667471deb9cd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "831d2a76-c151-48b7-85e7-d52b685b091e",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Highlight that sorting rules shape visibility and trust. Connect the app store/feed example to the idea that we can design different sorting pipelines, and those choices have fairness impacts.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "831d2a76-c151-48b7-85e7-d52b685b091e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a4f59a91-34c1-484f-a6e1-667471deb9cd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e6b4bc86-34e8-45b9-91f2-f45c7bee7aed",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Use a concrete card set to explore how the order of filter and sort affects efficiency.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Briefly introduce the MyTube card activity. Explain that students will build two different sequences (filter → sort and sort → filter) and then compare them for effort and fairness.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Lesson 10, Level 1. Walk through the instructions for Step One and Two. Emphasize using only the visible Sports tag for filtering and the exact scoring rule score = likes − dislikes + subscribers for ranking. Remind students not to add or change tags unless you explicitly say so.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students are correctly applying the filter first, then sorting only the remaining cards. Ask: How are you deciding which card has the highest score? Did any videos that look like sports disappear because they lacked the Sports tag? Prompt them to think about missing or misapplied labels.\n",
        "progression_name": "Introduction to Filter & Sort",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "You may choose to have students complete this activity in an unplugged fashion using this Intro to Filter & Sort - Activity Guide version of the same activity contained in Level 1. If students struggle with the score, have them compute it for just two or three cards together, writing scores on sticky notes before ranking.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e6b4bc86-34e8-45b9-91f2-f45c7bee7aed",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1cb26648-c7df-4932-85e5-8233fce4c7bd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "34c2bf79-05cb-4388-a6db-966980e2e201",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "34c2bf79-05cb-4388-a6db-966980e2e201",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1cb26648-c7df-4932-85e5-8233fce4c7bd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3d305fbf-8420-499b-b502-0d66a890678a",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Considering both effort and fairness, explain which algorithm felt better overall: filter → sort or sort → filter\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Surface that filter → sort usually takes less work because sorting a smaller set is “lighter,” and that both sequences can still hide mis-labeled items. Steer students to notice that good pipelines think about both efficiency and fairness, not just one.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3d305fbf-8420-499b-b502-0d66a890678a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1cb26648-c7df-4932-85e5-8233fce4c7bd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "729d3c0e-e7a8-4acd-9ce4-9cf87e387d97",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define pipeline. Explain that a pipeline is a series of steps where each step takes the output of the previous one and processes it further. Clarify that this is also called “chaining,” meaning the steps are connected and run one after another. Briefly note that because each step builds on the previous one, the order of steps will matter—this will be explored on the next slide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk students through the example pipeline step by step. Explain what happens to the data at each stage (filter, then sort, then map). Emphasize that each step changes the data before the next step runs, so the final result depends on how these steps are combined.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "729d3c0e-e7a8-4acd-9ce4-9cf87e387d97",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1cb26648-c7df-4932-85e5-8233fce4c7bd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dd3ade5e-096b-4a5f-8d1d-44fe9a2240cb",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk students through the pipeline step by step, focusing on how the data changes at each stage. Emphasize that each step transforms the data before the next step runs, so the final result depends on how these steps are ordered. Connect this back to the earlier activity by reminding students that switching the order (filter → sort vs sort → filter) changed the outcome. Highlight that pipelines are not just sequences of steps—they are combinations of rules that shape what users ultimately see.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Use this example to show how the data flows through each step: first it is filtered, then sorted, then rendered. Emphasize that this is an example of a pipeline, where each function takes the result of the previous one. Connect this back to the earlier activity by pointing out that changing the order of these steps (for example, sorting before filtering) would produce a different result. Highlight that each step applies a rule, and these rules combine to shape what users ultimately see.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dd3ade5e-096b-4a5f-8d1d-44fe9a2240cb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1cb26648-c7df-4932-85e5-8233fce4c7bd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "462f5ea3-e5ac-4e9f-9cef-b48517e32bb9",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 10, Level 1 to complete Levels 2 through 5.\n- Level 2: Students explore sorting and how it changes the order of a list.\n- Level 3: Students build a filter → sort → render pipeline to control which items appear and how they are ordered.\n- Level 4: Students pick a scenario and implement a filter → sort → render pipeline in Web Lab.\n- Level 5: Students write a free-response reflection about how their pipeline’s sort and map choices affect who gets seen and how fair it feels.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Help students choose an option that interests them and fits their skill level. As they work, check that their plain-language AI prompt planning matches their code and that their test tables include tricky edge cases (missing labels, low engagement, borderline ages, bot bursts, etc.). Ask: How could this rule unintentionally hide or over-promote certain people or content?\n",
        "progression_name": "Design A Pipeline",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time is tight, allow pairs to collaborate on one pipeline but have each student complete their own reflection on Level 5 so you can assess individual understanding.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "462f5ea3-e5ac-4e9f-9cef-b48517e32bb9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1cb26648-c7df-4932-85e5-8233fce4c7bd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "397b320c-2241-4832-86b4-07d13c36718b",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "397b320c-2241-4832-86b4-07d13c36718b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1cb26648-c7df-4932-85e5-8233fce4c7bd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "93c8f07f-2ddc-4aa2-b736-c7140e76bed6",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Ask students to write a brief exit reflection (on paper or digitally) naming one concrete improvement they would make to their pipeline for fairness or representation.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "93c8f07f-2ddc-4aa2-b736-c7140e76bed6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "36668a05-f190-4c6e-879f-99846a75a1f1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "116cc9c7-4c6b-4bf6-9a6e-c58d01c53347",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *What's an app or website that helps you explore or compare information?*\n- *What makes it helpful, fair, or easy to use?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students identify qualities of apps that present data clearly and fairly, grounding today’s work in what makes information accessible and meaningful.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "116cc9c7-4c6b-4bf6-9a6e-c58d01c53347",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9621c3d6-ede1-437a-9ce9-f0c3f9e38d8f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "89bd190e-5fc7-4d44-9f86-0dee0ecb0aee",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the first part of the project requirements.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Values Explorer Project Rubric\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What do you notice about the project requirements and rubric? What does success look like for this project?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students share what they notice about the project requirements and rubric and identify the components to implement in their program.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the milestone structure of the project. Explain that the Values Explorer Project is divided into four phases: planning the idea and data, building the interface, adding interactive features, and polishing the final app. Use the visual roadmap to preview how students will move from an idea to a working app they can share.\n",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that milestones help them focus on one part of the project at a time. At each milestone, students should ask: What have I planned or built so far? What needs to work next? What should I improve before moving on?\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "89bd190e-5fc7-4d44-9f86-0dee0ecb0aee",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9621c3d6-ede1-437a-9ce9-f0c3f9e38d8f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fd9b96b2-b782-43c6-a7da-e52ba4e2a093",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fd9b96b2-b782-43c6-a7da-e52ba4e2a093",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9621c3d6-ede1-437a-9ce9-f0c3f9e38d8f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d6e39844-00ab-4b88-9731-51b50d1a377f",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that today's focus is Milestone 1: Plan Your Value Explorer. Students will define their project idea, choose a value to explore, plan their dataset, and begin shaping the data structure for their app.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to focus on making a clear starting plan rather than trying to perfect every detail. Their ideas, schema, and dataset can change as they build and test their app.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d6e39844-00ab-4b88-9731-51b50d1a377f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9621c3d6-ede1-437a-9ce9-f0c3f9e38d8f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "96e07230-b351-4a69-a7fc-dfde62e41975",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "96e07230-b351-4a69-a7fc-dfde62e41975",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9621c3d6-ede1-437a-9ce9-f0c3f9e38d8f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a7f8d950-0c1c-45cb-84e0-f0fd0971c02a",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 11, Levels 1-3. Explain that these levels will help them plan their Value Explorer idea, sketch a schema, and create a first version of their schema and dataset.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, ask guiding questions such as:\n- “Which value does your project focus on?”\n- “What kinds of items will be in your dataset?”\n- “What information does each item need?”\n\nUse these check-ins to confirm students are creating a clear starting point for their dataset before moving into the interface-building levels.\n",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 1",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to focus on clarity rather than polish. Their first schema and dataset do not need to be perfect; they should be strong enough to guide the next stage of building.\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Set expectations for pacing. Levels 1 - 3 can typically be finished by the end of today’s class or early next class. Students who finish early can review their dataset for missing perspectives or revise entries that do not fit their chosen value."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a7f8d950-0c1c-45cb-84e0-f0fd0971c02a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9621c3d6-ede1-437a-9ce9-f0c3f9e38d8f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "90099367-3018-4448-8eac-024087ba775a",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "90099367-3018-4448-8eac-024087ba775a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9621c3d6-ede1-437a-9ce9-f0c3f9e38d8f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "22387e01-8919-40d8-ab91-0ee4f65d7b5d",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to Level 4 to complete the Milestone 1 Reflection.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Encourage students to think about whose perspectives are represented in their dataset and who might be missing.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 1 Reflection",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time permits, invite a few volunteers to share aloud or conduct a quick pair-and-share to close."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "22387e01-8919-40d8-ab91-0ee4f65d7b5d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9621c3d6-ede1-437a-9ce9-f0c3f9e38d8f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3886b51f-fea4-42d6-ad0c-fd93b3ce7fd1",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3886b51f-fea4-42d6-ad0c-fd93b3ce7fd1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9621c3d6-ede1-437a-9ce9-f0c3f9e38d8f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "52c16936-eadb-432d-b30e-e42f394e2aac",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts:\n- *What's one part of your dataset that you're most excited to display visually?*\n- *What's one thing you're not sure how to show clearly yet?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students reflect on data display challenges, preparing for wireframing and UI design decisions.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "52c16936-eadb-432d-b30e-e42f394e2aac",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4001b81f-2ca8-49ba-91e3-1a7a112bcc93"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d5bc68e7-0ef5-4ffc-b50a-4412fa60efa7",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the tasks for Day 2. Explain that students should finish any remaining work from Levels 1–4, then begin Milestone 2 by planning and building how their dataset will appear in the app.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 11, Levels 5–6. Explain that students will shift from planning their data to designing and building how that data appears on the page. In Level 5, they will sketch the page layout and card design. In Level 6, they will turn that plan into a first working interface that renders data cards using .map().\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ensure students are moving from design into a working first draft. Ask check-in questions like:\n- “Which schema fields will appear on your card?”\n- “Are the labels clear for each field?”\n- “Does each item in your dataset create one card?”\n- “How will users scan or compare the information?”\n\nProvide quick support if students get stuck, and encourage them to use Page Design Chat or AI Tutor for help refining layout, labels, or rendering logic.\n",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 2"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d5bc68e7-0ef5-4ffc-b50a-4412fa60efa7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4001b81f-2ca8-49ba-91e3-1a7a112bcc93"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e8dc1244-b18c-4eff-885c-e13d2cb83e8c",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to Level 7 to reflect on the clarity and accessibility of the interface they just created.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students write and discuss, listen for reflections that describe how their layout, labels, headings, and card design help users understand the data. Encourage students to identify one specific change they made or could make to improve clarity or accessibility.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 2 Reflection",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time permits, invite a few students to share one design choice that makes their data easier to understand. Highlight that clear design helps users read, compare, and trust the information in an app.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e8dc1244-b18c-4eff-885c-e13d2cb83e8c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4001b81f-2ca8-49ba-91e3-1a7a112bcc93"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0b6376ee-eb17-4ba2-8c8a-a27d16edf0cd",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What’s something in your app that isn’t showing the data the way you expected? What do you think might be causing it?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students practice articulating how their code transforms or displays data, normalizing debugging as an ongoing part of algorithm design.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0b6376ee-eb17-4ba2-8c8a-a27d16edf0cd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2c7d6a4c-bbf0-44a7-bc09-1d282a87d3cd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e81c84a3-e2e3-4628-bb25-24a5f2cdfb76",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the tasks for Day 3. Explain that students will finish any remaining interface work, then begin Milestone 3 by adding search, filter, or sort features that help users explore their dataset.\n",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 3",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Point out that students are moving from displaying data to making that data interactive. Encourage them to focus on getting one feature working clearly first, then test it, improve it, and consider how it affects what users see.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e81c84a3-e2e3-4628-bb25-24a5f2cdfb76",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2c7d6a4c-bbf0-44a7-bc09-1d282a87d3cd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a6fd38e4-4398-4c4d-8b20-68b21e5b83bb",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a6fd38e4-4398-4c4d-8b20-68b21e5b83bb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2c7d6a4c-bbf0-44a7-bc09-1d282a87d3cd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "27ae6e62-e675-4b1a-999e-a291045183ad",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 11, Level 8. Explain that students will now make their data display more interactive so users can explore the dataset in different ways. In this level, students will choose at least two features to add: search, filter, or sort. These features should help users find, narrow down, or organize the information shown on the page.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ensure students are connecting their interactive features to the structure and purpose of their dataset. Ask check-in questions like:\n\n- “Which two features are you adding: search, filter, or sort?”\n- “What field or fields does your search look through?”\n- “What category or condition are you using for your filter?”\n- “What field makes the most sense to sort by?”\n- “How will users know what each control does?”\n- “Did you test your feature with more than one input or choice?”\n- “Does the display update correctly when the user searches, filters, or sorts?”\n\nProvide quick support if students get stuck, especially with connecting inputs, dropdowns, or buttons to their data-rendering logic. Encourage students to use AI Tutor to help design and code each feature, debug issues, and generate three ways to test whether their features are working correctly.\n\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "27ae6e62-e675-4b1a-999e-a291045183ad",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2c7d6a4c-bbf0-44a7-bc09-1d282a87d3cd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c6393059-0471-4152-bac9-ade46c43581a",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c6393059-0471-4152-bac9-ade46c43581a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2c7d6a4c-bbf0-44a7-bc09-1d282a87d3cd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e2cce364-c606-4962-bc15-26ae3054e683",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain the TAG Peer Review process and model what thoughtful feedback looks like. Have students pair up or form small groups and TAG one to two peers' projects. They can leave feedback on sticky notes (digital or paper) directly at their peer's workspace. Emphasize that this is not a final critique — it's a chance to gather early insights as part of Milestone 3.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may be at different points when it's time for peer review. Reassure them that work doesn't need to be complete to be reviewed — feedback during the process helps improve the final product.\nReinforce the mindset that design and development are iterative, and peer feedback offers new perspectives and guidance.\nIf time allows, invite a few students to share one piece of feedback they received that will guide their next steps.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e2cce364-c606-4962-bc15-26ae3054e683",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2c7d6a4c-bbf0-44a7-bc09-1d282a87d3cd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "830c2276-3543-4e1c-aad7-4e902b30bacc",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "830c2276-3543-4e1c-aad7-4e902b30bacc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2c7d6a4c-bbf0-44a7-bc09-1d282a87d3cd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a86e67de-0fc5-4be7-ac61-3130e5bce24d",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to Level 9 to reflect on the search, filter, or sort features they added. Students should identify one fix or improvement they made, one feature they might add next, and how their feature choices affect which items users see, hide, or prioritize.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, invite a few students to share one thing they fixed after testing. Highlight that interactive features are not neutral — search, filter, and sort choices can shape what users notice first and who or what might be left out.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a86e67de-0fc5-4be7-ac61-3130e5bce24d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2c7d6a4c-bbf0-44a7-bc09-1d282a87d3cd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "44e0ce2d-ffbd-4e4e-941f-d94d85866dc5",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "44e0ce2d-ffbd-4e4e-941f-d94d85866dc5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2c7d6a4c-bbf0-44a7-bc09-1d282a87d3cd"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1aa15c6b-f3b9-4846-9617-334f7b6152de",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *If someone were testing your app right now, what’s the one thing you’d want them to notice first?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students reflect on the purpose and user experience of their app, setting an intentional focus for peer testing and feedback during today’s class.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1aa15c6b-f3b9-4846-9617-334f7b6152de",
        "lesson_activity.key": "facaa30c-d4c0-4438-ba46-b13fd345fb28"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3953ad85-4b42-4c99-a27c-060193a8fa7b",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the tasks for Day 4. Explain that students will finish any remaining Milestone 3 work, then begin Milestone 4 by polishing their app and reflecting on their final Value Explorer.\n\nStudents should focus on making their app clearer, more useful, and more reliable before sharing it.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain the TAG Peer Review process and model what thoughtful feedback looks like. Have students pair up or form small groups and TAG one to two peers' projects. They can leave feedback on sticky notes (digital or paper) directly at their peer's workspace. Emphasize that this is not a final critique — it's a chance to gather early insights as part of Milestone 3.\n",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 4",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may be at different points when it's time for peer review. Reassure them that work doesn't need to be complete to be reviewed — feedback during the process helps improve the final product.\nReinforce the mindset that design and development are iterative, and peer feedback offers new perspectives and guidance.\nIf time allows, invite a few students to share one piece of feedback they received that will guide their next steps.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3953ad85-4b42-4c99-a27c-060193a8fa7b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "facaa30c-d4c0-4438-ba46-b13fd345fb28"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0b6bc01e-5ed2-46f4-90ca-2d9143f63bff",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0b6bc01e-5ed2-46f4-90ca-2d9143f63bff",
        "lesson_activity.key": "facaa30c-d4c0-4438-ba46-b13fd345fb28"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4b6754a1-5efc-44cf-88a8-f0376f3bb6fa",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to revisit Level 9 if they made more progress on their search, filter, or sort features. They should update their reflection with any new fixes, improvements, or questions that came up during testing.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to notice what changed after more testing. Emphasize that improving interactive features often means checking what works, fixing what does not, and thinking carefully about how those choices affect what users see.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4b6754a1-5efc-44cf-88a8-f0376f3bb6fa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "facaa30c-d4c0-4438-ba46-b13fd345fb28"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3f90fad7-1722-4899-b033-3c4d3ffe695b",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3f90fad7-1722-4899-b033-3c4d3ffe695b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "facaa30c-d4c0-4438-ba46-b13fd345fb28"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4f800f6d-44d5-4641-84e5-02ae75924e60",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What’s one thing you still need to check or polish before your app feels ready to share?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students identify final improvement tasks related to clarity, accessibility, fairness, or data accuracy as they prepare to finalize their Values Explorer app.\n",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up",
        "progression_name": "Milestone Three"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4f800f6d-44d5-4641-84e5-02ae75924e60",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3711b1c5-21ef-487d-a463-6c2246aaf566"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e05b1afa-b67a-4528-9d3d-85a2e31bd682",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the tasks for Day 5. Explain that today students will finish Milestone 4 by making final improvements, checking that their app works, and preparing to share their Value Explorer.\n\nRemind students to focus on the changes that will have the biggest impact on clarity, usability, and trust.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to their Values Explorer Project Rubric. Have students review each category carefully and mark where they believe their project currently falls. Encourage honesty — the goal is to identify strengths and areas for continued growth as intentional designers.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students are engaging thoughtfully with each rubric criterion, not rushing to completion.\n",
        "duration": "30",
        "name": "Activity",
        "progression_name": "Milestone Four",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that self-assessment isn't just about scoring their project — it's a professional habit that helps them take ownership of their design choices. Say something like: \"Designers constantly evaluate their own work to make sure it aligns with their goals, audience, and purpose. This rubric helps you see where you've grown and what you can still refine.\"\n\nIf time allows, have students highlight one category they feel especially proud of and one they'd like to improve next time.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e05b1afa-b67a-4528-9d3d-85a2e31bd682",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3711b1c5-21ef-487d-a463-6c2246aaf566"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6bbd9384-abc3-4f0d-8173-6cdb52c6982e",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6bbd9384-abc3-4f0d-8173-6cdb52c6982e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3711b1c5-21ef-487d-a463-6c2246aaf566"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f9699651-2a7f-4ada-91ef-0c09eaedb88f",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Level 10 to make final improvements to their Value Explorer. Explain that this is their final dedicated work session before sharing. Reinforce that today’s goal is to prioritize the changes that will have the biggest impact, rather than trying to fix everything at once.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ensure students are on task and testing their apps. Ask:\n- “Does your dataset render correctly?”\n- “Do your search, filter, or sort features work?”\n- “What is one improvement that would make your app clearer or easier to use?”\n- “What final change will have the biggest impact for your users?”\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that final polish is about making smart choices with the time they have. Encourage them to focus on clarity, usability, reliability, and trust.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f9699651-2a7f-4ada-91ef-0c09eaedb88f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3711b1c5-21ef-487d-a463-6c2246aaf566"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "635ff1ad-f2f0-4f16-8c51-5b247d34de59",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "635ff1ad-f2f0-4f16-8c51-5b247d34de59",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3711b1c5-21ef-487d-a463-6c2246aaf566"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8f64e456-9743-4546-a528-e0cd31949c14",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Distribute several sticky notes to each student for the gallery walk.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students begin the Gallery Walk by displaying their Values Explorer Apps on their computers, then moving around the room to explore classmates' projects. As they visit each one, students should use their sticky notes to leave feedback, kudos, or questions following the TAG approach (Tell, Ask, Give). Encourage them to engage with a range of projects—not just those of close friends.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Move around the room to observe how students are engaging with the displayed projects. Encourage students to leave a note about what they find interesting or insightful about their peers' work. Ensure that students are thoughtfully engaging with a variety of projects, not just those of their closest peers.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Frame the Gallery Walk as a celebration of learning and creativity, not a critique. Say something like: \"This is your chance to see how everyone's ideas came to life in different ways. You might even get inspired by someone else's approach!\"\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8f64e456-9743-4546-a528-e0cd31949c14",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3711b1c5-21ef-487d-a463-6c2246aaf566"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fd96c0ad-cb21-487c-a7dd-c11fddbdc7a9",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You’ve gone from choosing a value and planning your data to building, debugging, refining, and polishing your app. This final reflection helps you capture how your app changed, how AI Tutor supported your work, and how your design choices affect fairness, clarity, and user trust.\n",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fd96c0ad-cb21-487c-a7dd-c11fddbdc7a9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3711b1c5-21ef-487d-a463-6c2246aaf566"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d7f5ffbb-784a-420b-a968-3077ee97c222",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to Lesson 11, Level 11 to complete the final reflection. Explain that this final step helps them look back at their process and name what they learned from building and improving their Value Explorer.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Encourage students to include specific examples from their project, such as a feature they revised, a bug they fixed, a design choice they made, or a moment when AI Tutor helped them move forward. If students finish early, prompt them to review their app one more time or support a peer who is finalizing their project.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use the Final Reflection as evidence for students’ process, iteration, AI use, and ethical reasoning about fairness, clarity, and user trust.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d7f5ffbb-784a-420b-a968-3077ee97c222",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3711b1c5-21ef-487d-a463-6c2246aaf566"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "51f29520-98a6-43ef-b9a3-d56ec557f1f1",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "End of Unit Assessment"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "51f29520-98a6-43ef-b9a3-d56ec557f1f1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "2ba735d9-f531-4633-875b-4e5f375e5ec5"
      }
    }
  ],
